2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminomalononitrile\u2013acetaldehyde addition product\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxyethyl-substituted aminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoethanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible cyanohydrin formation from acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, concentrated surface water at proposed pH 7\u20138.5. An illustrative screening inventory is 5\u201320 mM acetaldehyde and 10\u201350 mM total cyanide; these are design values, not a reported threonine-specific recipe. Follow cyanohydrin formation over 0.5\u201324 h rather than assuming complete conversion. Acetaldehyde and HCN volatilization require rapid trapping or replenishment. Transfer the lactonitrile-containing water directly into the photoreduction stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds reversibly to the acetaldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer to give lactonitrile. No externally supplied mineral catalyst is required.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the experimentally grounded entry to the lactaldehyde branch in Ritson and Sutherland (2013), subsequently incorporated into Patel et al. (2015). The equilibrium, volatility losses and substrate concentration must be measured in the actual water composition. Toner and Catling (2019) support possible cyanide concentration and storage settings, not guaranteed coavailability of concentrated acetaldehyde.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu(I)-assisted sulfide photoreduction of lactonitrile to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"CuCN/Cu(I) photoredox catalyst\",\n        \"Phosphate buffer\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K proposed bulk-water window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN/Cu(I), with 10 mol% as the representative experimental starting point\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, optically thin water irradiated at 254 nm for a 2 h benchmark exposure near pH 7. The reported representative photoredox formulation used 10 mM nitrile, 30 mM H2S, 33 mM phosphate and 10 mol% CuCN; that formulation was described for glycolonitrile and must not be mistaken for a separately verified complete lactonitrile recipe. Avoid prolonged irradiation after aldehyde formation. A candidate natural arrangement is shallow water above settled FeS-rich sediment, with dissolved Fe2+, copper speciation, sulfide depletion and UV transmission explicitly measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV-driven reducing chemistry converts the nitrile through a reduced nitrogen-containing intermediate; hydrolysis releases the aldehyde and ammonia. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. S8 is a candidate terminal S(0) bookkeeping sink; actual sulfur products can include polysulfides and sulfur\u2013cyanide species.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) reported lactaldehyde in 19% yield after 2 h and subsequent access to the threonine-family aminonitrile. This measured precursor yield is not a free-threonine yield. The catalyst and buffer are justified by the demonstrated implementation, rather than generic mineral plausibility. Ranjan et al. (2018) identify sulfide precipitation and UV attenuation as serious deployment constraints. The S8 output closes a possible net redox balance but is not asserted to be an experimentally quantified exclusive sulfur product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactaldehyde capture as its cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic water at proposed pH 7\u20139, with renewed cyanide supply after photoreduction. Use millimolar lactaldehyde and cyanide in molar excess as an integration test, not as a claimed natural inventory. Capture should follow aldehyde generation without prolonged storage. Measure the aldehyde/cyanohydrin distribution over hours; neither quantitative capture nor a specific environmental lifetime is established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible nucleophilic addition of cyanide to lactaldehyde produces 2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile. Multiple stereoisomers are possible.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The cyanohydrin intermediate is part of the lactaldehyde-to-aminonitrile sequence described by Ritson and Sutherland (2013). Keeping it explicit avoids collapsing carbonyl capture and aminonitrile formation into an instantaneous Strecker edge. Lactaldehyde instability and competition with other carbonyl reactions remain important kinetic uncertainties.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to Strecker aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded pool or rock-covered channel. Proposed integration window: pH 8\u20139.5, 1\u201310 mM aldehyde-derived carbon and 50\u2013200 mM total ammonia/ammonium. These concentrations are screening assumptions; the supplied report establishes excess ammonia but not this exact recipe. Allow days to weeks, with 7\u201328 days as a monitoring window. Ammonia loss, competing sulfur chemistry and carbonyl degradation must be included. Transfer to an acidic hydrolysis setting only after measuring aminonitrile accumulation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The cyanohydrin equilibrates with lactaldehyde and cyanide. Ammonia forms an imine/iminium intermediate, which is captured by cyanide. The net transformation replaces the cyanohydrin hydroxyl functionality with amino functionality; it is not assumed to be direct hydroxide displacement.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) demonstrated formation of the threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile after adding HCN and excess ammonia; related conversions required days to weeks. The record therefore includes a substantial residence time and separates this stage from photoreduction. Neither enantioselectivity nor a quantitative threonine:allothreonine ratio is established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"First hydrolytic step: threonine-family aminonitrile to amide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330\u2013370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed environmental release stage at pH 1\u20132, potentially supplied by volcanic HCl without molecular oxygen. The aminonitrile-bearing fluid must be transported into an acidic drainage or pool; spontaneous acidification of an ammoniacal reservoir is not assumed. Track nitrile, amide and degradation products over weeks to months. This is an observation window, not a validated conversion time. In acidic water, amino groups are protonated although neutral formula equivalents are used in the graph.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration gives the corresponding carboxamide. The amide is a conventional mechanistic intermediate and need not accumulate to isolable concentrations.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) establish the aminonitrile precursor, not environmentally efficient free-threonine release. Nitrile-to-amide hydration is established chemistry, but this particular environmental operating window and its rate are speculative in the supplied evidence. Explicitly resolving the amide prevents the hydrolysis bottleneck from being hidden. No mineral is added without target-specific justification.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Second hydrolytic step: threonine-family amide to free amino acids\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350\u2013370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1\u20132 hydrolysis in UV-shielded acidic water, monitored over weeks to months. Determine amide disappearance, threonine formation and threonine destruction independently. Cool collected amino-acid-bearing water below about 330 K after release where possible. Strong laboratory hydrolysis is a benchmark only; the network does not assume that pH 1\u20132 reproduces a 6 M HCl workup. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium at this pH.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis releases the carboxylic acid and ammonia/ammonium. Hydrolysis preserves the carbon skeleton but does not select the biological threonine stereoisomer. Both listed amino-acid outputs represent a mixed precursor population, not an imposed 1:1 product ratio.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the chemically necessary completion of the aminonitrile route discussed by Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015), but mild environmental completion was not demonstrated in the supplied findings. The proposed conditions and residence time are therefore explicitly speculative. The route is not considered environmentally productive until measured formation exceeds hydrolysis-stage and subsequent product destruction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminomalononitrile-forming channel within HCN oligomerization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ammonia/ammonium as an acid-base promoter\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Base promotion by ammonia/cyanide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed concentrated alkaline-water window: pH 8.5\u20139.5 and 0.1\u20131 M total cyanide, with ammonia used to establish ammoniacal conditions. Follow the mixture over hours to days and intercept aminomalononitrile before further oligomerization. Neither this concentration range nor a selective trimer yield is established for a natural site. Evaporative concentration or release from a cyanide reservoir is required; uncontaminated ocean water is not assumed to supply this inventory.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped trimer-forming channel produces aminomalononitrile from HCN. It competes with dimer, tetramer and higher-polymer chemistry and is not represented as a selective elementary three-body reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminomalononitrile is the HCN trimer used in Thanassi (1975), while Lowe et al. (1963) establish the broader complexity of ammonium-cyanide chemistry. Toner and Catling (2019) provide a plausible concentration/storage framework, not a demonstrated selective aminomalononitrile supply. Retaining enough reactive trimer is a major upstream bottleneck, and the proposed operating window is an integration hypothesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon\u2013carbon addition of aminomalononitrile to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately neutral anoxic water, with pH 6.5\u20137.5 as a proposed test window. An illustrative inventory is 5\u201320 mM aminomalononitrile and comparable or excess acetaldehyde, monitored over hours to days. Exact concentrations and residence times are not supplied for the historical threonine experiment. Introduce acetaldehyde promptly after trimer generation and account for competing reactions in unpurified HCN oligomer mixtures.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The activated carbon of aminomalononitrile forms a carbon\u2013carbon bond to the acetaldehyde carbonyl carbon, giving the hydroxyethyl-substituted dinitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thanassi (1975) directly supports threonine/allothreonine recovery after this aldehyde-addition chemistry and subsequent hydrolysis. Kuroda et al. (2020) describe the acetaldehyde reaction as occurring under almost neutral aqueous conditions. No mineral or metal catalyst is required. The accessible evidence does not justify an invented threonine percentage or a claim that crude geological mixtures behave like purified aminomalononitrile.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of the substituted aminomalononitrile to an aminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350\u2013370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed environmental hydrolysis at pH 1\u20132 after transport from the near-neutral addition setting. Follow both nitrile groups, transient amides, decarboxylation products and substrate loss over weeks to months. The listed diacid is a mechanistic node and need not accumulate before decarboxylation. Historical acid workup demonstrates releasability but does not establish these weaker-acid kinetics.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Both nitrile groups undergo hydrolysis through amide intermediates to carboxyl groups, releasing ammonia/ammonium. The resulting substituted aminomalonic acid is poised for decarboxylation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thanassi (1975) supports the combined hydrolysis/decarboxylation endpoint, and Kuroda et al. (2020) discuss this interpretation. Independent isolation of this diacid and environmental rates are not established by the supplied report. Separating hydrolysis from carbon loss is a mechanistic representation, not a claim of independently demonstrated preparative steps; the proposed environmental release window is speculative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Decarboxylation of hydroxyethyl-substituted aminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330\u2013370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic to weakly acidic water, proposed pH 1\u20134. This stage can overlap with dinitrile hydrolysis rather than requiring storage of an isolated diacid. Monitor over days to weeks, with no validated environmental half-life assumed. Collect and cool the amino-acid-bearing fraction rather than extending hot residence after substrate depletion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of one carboxyl group as CO2 followed by protonation converts the substituted aminomalonic acid into the threonine/allothreonine skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis followed by decarboxylation explains the D,L-threonine and allo-threonine endpoint reported by Thanassi (1975) and summarized by Kuroda et al. (2020). The endpoint is experimentally supported, but the proposed surface-temperature kinetics and isolated intermediate behavior remain unmeasured. No threonine:allothreonine or L:D selectivity is imposed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed anoxic, shaded-water window at pH 8\u20139.5. Illustrative starting concentrations are 1\u201310 mM formaldehyde, 10\u201350 mM total cyanide and 50\u2013200 mM total ammonia/ammonium. Monitor over hours to days. These values are a screening design, not a recipe demonstrated in the supplied threonine papers. Competing formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry and HCN loss must be quantified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia generate a methanimine/iminium population that is trapped by cyanide to form aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This supplies glycine through standard Strecker chemistry rather than introducing glycine as an unexplained complex feedstock. The supplied report supports the Strecker reaction class through Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and the broader precursor-network context through Patel et al. (2015), but it does not supply a measured formaldehyde-specific recipe here; the integrated operating conditions are therefore speculative. This is not the unsupported generic aldehyde/ammonia-to-threonine edge contradicted by Koga and Naraoka (2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330\u2013370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1\u20132 acidic-water stage after transfer from ammoniacal Strecker water. Monitor aminoacetonitrile, glycinamide and side products over days to months. The observation period does not establish a conversion time. Natural transport must overcome the alkalinity of the incoming water before the hydrolysis conditions apply.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the nitrile gives glycinamide through the ordinary nitrile-hydration mechanism.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is conventional nitrile hydrolysis, included to expose the hydrolytic requirement of 