{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T12:22:13.812115", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Threonine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Threonine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Threonine: Routes, Intermediates, Catalysts, Environments, and Unresolved Problems\n\n## Scope and overall assessment\n\nThreonine is a comparatively difficult proteinogenic amino acid to account for prebiotically because its four-carbon skeleton contains **two adjacent stereogenic centers and a \u03b2-hydroxyl group**. The literature nevertheless contains several genuinely distinct routes to threonine or to chemically immediate threonine precursors. They span aqueous HCN chemistry, Strecker chemistry, aldehyde\u2013amino-acid condensation, atmospheric electrical discharge, ionizing-radiation chemistry, and extraterrestrial ice photochemistry. The evidentiary strength is very uneven: some experiments isolated or chromatographically identified threonine after hydrolysis, whereas others stopped at lactaldehyde or the threonine aminonitrile and therefore established a **precursor route rather than free threonine itself**.\n\nThe strongest threonine-specific conclusions from the primary literature are:\n\n| Route class | What has actually been demonstrated? | Main feedstocks | Evidentiary status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cyanosulfidic/Kiliani\u2013Fischer\u2013Strecker sequence | Lactaldehyde in **19% yield**, followed by conversion to the threonine/allo-threonine aminonitrile | HCN-derived nitriles, H\u2082S, NH\u2083, UV; Cu(I) in the original implementation | **Strong precursor-level demonstration**; free-threonine hydrolysis was not the central endpoint of the experiment. |\n| Aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde | D,L-threonine and allo-threonine after hydrolysis/decarboxylation | HCN trimer + CH\u2083CHO | **Direct experimental threonine route** under mild aqueous conditions. |\n| Glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde | Threonine among products | Glycine or glycine oligomers + CH\u2083CHO | **Direct experimental route**, catalyst-free, pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C, but poorly selective. |\n| Concentrated HCN/NH\u2083 polymer chemistry | Threonine released from hydrolyzed HCN-derived material | HCN or NH\u2083/HCN systems | **Historical direct detection after strong hydrolysis**; chemical identity of the immediate precursor is poorly constrained. |\n| \u03b3-radiolysis of aqueous HCN/cyanide | Threonine after hydrolysis of irradiated material | HCN, or NH\u2084CN/NaCN + ionizing radiation | **Direct post-hydrolysis detection**, independently reproduced in the 1970s. |\n| Electric-discharge chemistry | Threonine in the H\u2082S-rich 1958 Miller experiment | CH\u2084, NH\u2083, CO\u2082, H\u2082S, H\u2082O + spark | **Direct product-level detection**, but pathway and environmental realism are uncertain. |\n| UV photolysis of interstellar ice analogues | Threonine and allothreonine after ice photochemistry/workup | H\u2082O, C\u2082H\u2085OH, HCN, NH\u2083 | **Direct laboratory production**, with Thr:Gly = 60:100 in one ethanol-ice experiment. |\n| PAH-containing astrophysical ice | Threonine detected at relative abundance 13.6 versus alanine = 100; quantum yield \\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\) | Naphthalene, H\u2082O, NH\u2083 + UV/EUV | **Direct detection**, but extremely low absolute conversion. |\n| Interstellar lactaldehyde synthesis | Lactaldehyde made from CO + ethanol ice | CO, C\u2082H\u2085OH + energetic electrons | **Upstream precursor only**; strongly relevant because lactaldehyde is the required C\u2083 aldehyde for Strecker threonine. |\n| Aldehyde/ammonia \u201cformose-like\u201d parent-body chemistry | Many hydroxy amino acids, but **not threonine/allo-threonine** except trace L-Thr attributed to contamination | HCHO, CH\u2083CHO, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, \u00b1 glycolaldehyde | **Important negative result** for threonine. |\n| \u201cComplex glycine precursor\u201d alkylation | Acetaldehyde proposed as the side-chain donor for Thr | Irradiation-derived glycine precursor + CH\u2083CHO | **Proposed, not demonstrated threonine synthesis**; currently speculative. |\n\nTwo distinctions are crucial for synthesis-network design. First, **\u201cthreonine detected after 6 M HCl hydrolysis\u201d is not equivalent to \u201cfree threonine formed in the prebiotic reactor.\u201d** Several HCN, radiation, and ice experiments instead generate nitriles, oligomers, or other bound precursors that release threonine analytically. Second, the best-defined carbon-skeleton chemistry converges repeatedly on **acetaldehyde, lactaldehyde, glycine, or HCN oligomers**, making these more useful nodes for a reaction-network model than \u201cthreonine formation\u201d treated as a single black-box edge.\n\n## Known synthesis routes and their mechanisms\n\n### Cyanosulfidic photoredox chemistry through lactaldehyde\n\nThe most mechanistically explicit modern route was developed by **Dougal Ritson and John Sutherland (2013, *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*)** and incorporated into the broader cyanosulfidic network by **Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*)**. Its central idea is a photochemical, reductive version of Kiliani\u2013Fischer homologation in which an HCN-derived nitrile is reduced to an aldehyde, the aldehyde adds another HCN unit, and the new cyanohydrin is reduced again.\n\nFor threonine, the chemically important branch is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CHO + HCN \\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CN}\n\\]\n\nacetaldehyde \u2192 **lactonitrile**, followed by photoreductive conversion of the nitrile carbon to an aldehyde:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CN \\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CHO}\n\\]\n\ngiving **lactaldehyde (2-hydroxypropanal)**. Lactaldehyde then enters ordinary Strecker chemistry:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CHO + HCN + NH_3\n\\rightleftharpoons\nCH_3CH(OH)CH(NH_2)CN}\n\\]\n\nfollowed, in principle, by nitrile hydrolysis to:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CH(NH_2)COOH}\n\\]\n\nwhich is threonine/allothreonine. Ritson and Sutherland explicitly obtained lactaldehyde from the acetaldehyde-cyanohydrin system in **19% yield after 2 h**, and subsequently showed that addition of HCN and excess NH\u2083 converted lactaldehyde through its cyanohydrin into the **(allo-)threonine Strecker aminonitrile**. Conversion of related aldehydes to aminonitriles proceeded over days to weeks.\n\nThe underlying photoreduction was demonstrated under approximately neutral aqueous conditions. In a representative first-stage experiment, Ritson and Sutherland used **10 mM glycolonitrile, 30 mM H\u2082S, 33 mM phosphate, 10 mol% CuCN, pH 7, and 254-nm irradiation for 2 h**, obtaining glycolaldehyde hydrate in **42% yield**. The same photochemical logic generated acetaldehyde and ultimately lactaldehyde. H\u2082S acts as a stoichiometric reductant while UV excitation produces the reducing chemistry; Cu(I) greatly facilitates the original photoredox implementation.\n\nPatel et al. subsequently showed that this chemistry belongs to a larger network in which HCN-derived carbon can furnish precursors of ribonucleotides, amino acids and lipids. Importantly, in H\u2082S-containing versions, Cu(I) is not always indispensable to reduction, so copper should be regarded as a demonstrated accelerator/photoredox component rather than an invariant stoichiometric requirement of every version of the network.\n\nA later alternative photoredox system was demonstrated by **Xu, Ritson, Ranjan, Todd, Sasselov and Sutherland (2018, *Chemical Communications*)**: UV irradiation of **ferrocyanide plus sulfite** efficiently drives HCN reductive homologation to simple sugars and amino-acid/hydroxy-acid precursors. This increases the geochemical flexibility of the reductive-homologation module, although Xu et al. did **not establish a new threonine endpoint comparable to Ritson and Sutherland's lactaldehyde/aminonitrile experiment**.\n\nThe strengths of this route are unusually clear intermediates, a measured lactaldehyde yield, aqueous conditions near neutral pH, and a common chemical network with other classes of prebiotic molecules. Its weaknesses are equally clear: the endpoint experimentally emphasized is the **aminonitrile precursor**, not an isolated yield of free threonine; the route requires coordinated supplies of cyanide, reductant, UV, ammonia and sometimes copper; and there are geochemical tensions between abundant sulfide and UV transmission, discussed below.\n\n### Aminomalononitrile plus acetaldehyde\n\nA chemically distinct HCN route was demonstrated by **John W. Thanassi (1975, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*)**. Aminomalononitrile, NH\u2082CH(CN)\u2082, is the **HCN trimer**. Thanassi found that it reacts with electrophiles, including aldehydes, under mild aqueous conditions; acid hydrolysis of the products yielded several amino acids, explicitly including **D,L-threonine and allo-threonine**.\n\nFor threonine the electrophile is **acetaldehyde**. The reaction can be represented schematically as nucleophilic C\u2013C bond formation at the activated carbon of aminomalononitrile:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NH_2CH(CN)_2 + CH_3CHO\n\\rightarrow\nNH_2C(CN)_2CH(OH)CH_3}\n\\]\n\nfollowed by conversion of both nitriles into carboxyl groups under acid hydrolysis and loss of one carboxyl group by decarboxylation:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NH_2C(COOH)_2CH(OH)CH_3\n\\rightarrow\nNH_2CH(COOH)CH(OH)CH_3 + CO_2}\n\\]\n\nA later experimental paper by **Kuroda et al. (2020, *Natural Product Communications*)**, while extending the aminomalononitrile concept to other amino acids, describes Thanassi's threonine reaction as occurring by **alkylation with acetaldehyde under almost neutral aqueous conditions**, followed by acid hydrolysis and decarboxylation. Thanassi's primary abstract reports that the products were quantitatively measured but the numerical threonine yield is not given in the accessible abstract, so it would be unsafe to invent a percentage.\n\nNo mineral or metal catalyst is required for the demonstrated C\u2013C-forming step. The route therefore has an attractive minimality: **HCN \u2192 HCN trimer; HCN trimer + acetaldehyde \u2192 threonine precursor; hydrolysis/decarboxylation \u2192 threonine**. Its main liabilities are upstream. It requires enough HCN to generate and retain aminomalononitrile, acetaldehyde must be available simultaneously, and the laboratory endpoint uses strong acid hydrolysis. It also produces threonine together with its allo stereoisomer rather than solving threonine's stereochemical-selection problem.\n\nFor network modeling, aminomalononitrile is particularly interesting because it provides a **C\u2082N\u2083 activated carbon synthon generated directly by HCN oligomerization**, rather than requiring lactaldehyde. It therefore constitutes a genuinely independent branch from the lactaldehyde/Strecker route even though both ultimately depend on cyanide chemistry.\n\n### Condensation of glycine with acetaldehyde\n\nA much less frequently discussed but experimentally important route was reported by **C. P. Ivanov and O. C. Ivanov (1983, *Origins of Life*)**. They reacted **glycine and glycine oligohomopeptides with formaldehyde and acetaldehyde** in homogeneous weakly acidic solution. The reactions were run at **pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C, explicitly in the absence of inorganic solids**. Among the products were the expected serine and **threonine**, together with alanine, glutamate, aspartate, norvaline, isoleucine and nonprotein hydroxy amino acids.\n\nThe simplest net threonine transformation is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{glycine + acetaldehyde \\rightarrow threonine/allothreonine}\n\\]\n\nThat C\u2013C connectivity is the reverse of the familiar retro-aldol cleavage of threonine to glycine plus acetaldehyde. The prebiotic experiment is especially significant because it does **not require cyanide in the threonine-forming step, metal minerals, UV or ionizing radiation**. Glycine can in principle be supplied by many other prebiotic routes, so this reaction creates a useful point of convergence between otherwise unrelated origin-of-life networks.\n\nThe trade-off is selectivity. The Ivanovs obtained a fairly broad amino-acid inventory, indicating that aldehyde\u2013amino-acid chemistry in this regime is not a selective threonine synthesis. The accessible primary abstract gives no isolated threonine percentage or threonine:allothreonine ratio. More fundamentally, the route moves rather than eliminates the precursor problem: it requires a pool containing **glycine or oligoglycine plus acetaldehyde at 60\u201380 \u00b0C**.\n\nNevertheless, among routes relevant to network design it deserves more attention than it commonly receives because it provides a direct edge:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{glycine}+\\text{acetaldehyde}\\rightarrow\\text{threonine-family products}}\n\\]\n\nunder catalyst-free aqueous conditions.\n\n### Bulk HCN and ammonia chemistry\n\nThreonine also appears in the older literature on **HCN oligomerization/polymerization**, where the immediate molecular precursor is much less well defined.\n\n**Lowe, Rees and Markham (1963, *Nature*)** demonstrated that ammonium cyanide produces a complex collection of amino acids, amino-acid polymers, fatty acids and purines. Closely related work by **Clifford Matthews and Robert Moser (1966\u20131967, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* and *Nature*)** showed that HCN-derived polymers and HCN/ammonia reaction products can, after hydrolysis, yield a broad suite of \u03b1-amino acids. Matthews and Moser's hydrolysates explicitly contained **threonine**, alongside glycine, serine, aspartate, histidine, lysine and other amino acids.\n\nThe key mechanistic point is that this is **not clean Strecker chemistry from a known carbonyl**. HCN produces dimers, trimers, tetramers and higher nitrogen-rich oligomers; water and ammonia then transform these networks into amide-, peptide- and amino-acid-like structures. Matthews and Moser emphasized peptide-like material that released amino acids only after strong hydrolysis. Thus the experiment proves that an HCN/NH\u2083 network can encode the threonine carbon skeleton, but does not identify a single dominant elementary pathway to it.\n\nThis ambiguity is important. A reactor that yields threonine only after **6 N HCl at approximately 100 \u00b0C for many hours** has demonstrated a hydrolyzable threonine precursor, not necessarily an environmental pool of free threonine. Moreover, classical HCN-polymer experiments commonly use very high cyanide concentrations and alkaline/ammoniacal conditions, making concentration a major prebiotic-plausibility issue. The chemistry is real; its geochemical deployment remains contested.\n\n### Ionizing-radiation chemistry of HCN\n\n**Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life*)** exposed **0.004\u20130.1 M aqueous HCN** to ^60Co \u03b3-radiation. After irradiation and hydrolysis they identified seven protein amino acids by chromatographic/mass-spectrometric methods: glycine, alanine, valine, serine, **threonine**, aspartate and glutamate. The reported ^60Co dose rate was approximately **200,000 rad h\u207b\u00b9**, equivalent to about **2 kGy h\u207b\u00b9**. Control experiments were used to exclude contamination.\n\n**Zorica Dragani\u0107, Ivan Dragani\u0107, Akira Shimoyama and Cyril Ponnamperuma (1977, *Origins of Life*)** subsequently irradiated oxygen-free aqueous HCN, NH\u2084CN and NaCN and concluded that the process is initiated by **free-radical chemistry producing oligomeric products that release amino acids on hydrolysis**. Chiral products were approximately racemic, and radiation-chemical amino-acid yields varied with radiation dose.\n\nThis route needs no mineral catalyst: the source of activation is ionizing radiation and water-derived/cyanide-derived radical chemistry. Its principal prebiotic uncertainty is therefore not chemical feasibility but **dosimetry**. A ^60Co source is a laboratory proxy, not a natural Hadean source; demonstrating that an equivalent dose can be supplied at useful rates by radionuclides, energetic particles, impact radiation or another natural process remains necessary before the laboratory route can be promoted to a quantitatively compelling terrestrial scenario. The analytical-hydrolysis caveat also applies: threonine was recovered after hydrolysis of irradiated products.\n\n### Electric-discharge synthesis\n\nThe best direct atmospheric-discharge evidence comes from the archived 1958 experiment reanalyzed by **Eric Parker and colleagues (2011, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*)**. Miller had charged a 5-L apparatus with approximately **258 mmHg CH\u2084, 100 mmHg H\u2082S, 250 mmHg NH\u2083 and 87 mmHg CO\u2082**, then subjected the recirculating system to electrical discharge. Modern UPLC and time-of-flight MS analysis found **23 amino acids and four amines**. Threonine, leucine and isoleucine were detected in this H\u2082S-rich experiment but not in the comparison classic, volcanic or silent-discharge sample sets examined by the same group.\n\nNo intentional mineral catalyst was present. However, Parker et al. noted sulfur deposition near the **tungsten electrodes**, leaving open the possibility that electrode/sulfur surface chemistry modified product distributions. Consequently, it is safest to describe the experiment as **spark-driven rather than rigorously catalyst-free**. The detailed pathway to threonine was not established; plausible chemistry includes formation of carbonyls, HCN and nitriles followed by aqueous reactions, but assigning a particular threonine mechanism would go beyond the experiment.\n\nThe result is nevertheless important because it demonstrates that threonine can emerge from a complex gas-phase/aqueous reaction network without supplying lactaldehyde or aminomalononitrile by hand. The drawback is environmental. The experiment is strongly reducing and unusually rich in CH\u2084, NH\u2083 and H\u2082S compared with most models of the global Hadean atmosphere. Parker et al. themselves therefore emphasized possible **local or transient reducing environments**, including volcanic settings, rather than requiring such a composition globally.\n\nA separate historical line of work by **Matthews and Moser (1966, *PNAS*)** subjected dry CH\u2084/NH\u2083 mixtures to electrical discharge and found threonine among amino acids released by hydrolysis of resulting peptide-like residues. This configuration differs chemically from the canonical Miller water-circulation experiment and again points to a complex hydrolyzable precursor network rather than a uniquely resolved threonine-forming elementary reaction.\n\n### Extraterrestrial ice photochemistry\n\nAstrophysical ice chemistry provides two separate direct threonine demonstrations.\n\n**Jamie Elsila, Jason Dworkin, Max Bernstein, Mildred Martin and Scott Sandford (2007, *The Astrophysical Journal*)** irradiated interstellar-ice analogues at approximately **20 K**. In an experiment replacing methanol with ethanol, the starting ice had composition:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_2O:C_2H_5OH:HCN:NH_3=20:2:1:1}.\n\\]\n\nThe overall amino-acid yield was lower than in the corresponding methanol system: glycine was only about **10% of the glycine yield in the methanol-containing ice**. But the carbon-chain diversity increased substantially. In the hydrolyzed residue, relative abundances normalized to glycine = 100 included **D,L-threonine = 60 and D,L-allothreonine = 30**, along with alanine = 26, \u03b2-alanine = 5, serine = 14 and several other products.\n\nThis is one of the strongest quantitative threonine results in the entire literature. It is also mechanistically sobering. Isotope-labeling experiments in the same study showed that amino-acid formation in UV-processed ices proceeds through **multiple pathways**, and for the compounds investigated in detail the dominant routes did not conform simply to either a textbook Strecker mechanism or the simplest radical\u2013radical scheme. Threonine's own detailed atom-by-atom pathway was not resolved.\n\nA second experiment by **Y.-J. Chen, Michel Nuevo and colleagues (2008, *Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society*)** irradiated a **1:1:1 naphthalene:H\u2082O:NH\u2083 ice at 15 K** with **4\u201320 eV photons**. Thirteen amino acids were identified. Alanine was most abundant at an extrapolated **2.2 nmol** in the entire sample; threonine had a relative abundance of **13.6 when alanine = 100** and an estimated photochemical quantum yield of **\\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\)**.\n\nThe naphthalene experiment is relevant to broad surveys because it demonstrates a threonine route that does **not begin with HCN as the supplied carbon source**. Its limitation is efficiency: only a tiny fraction of the PAH carbon entered identified amino acids, and many other organic photoproducts dominated the residue. The authors therefore interpreted it as evidence for multiple possible astrochemical mechanisms, not a selective threonine factory.\n\n### Interstellar production of the critical lactaldehyde precursor\n\nA major advance relevant to the Strecker route came from **Wang et al. (2024, *Nature Communications*)**, who directly synthesized **lactaldehyde** in an interstellar-ice analogue. A CO/ethanol ice at **5 K** was exposed to energetic electrons as proxies for galactic cosmic rays. The experimentally supported elementary step is radical\u2013radical combination:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HCO^\\bullet + CH_3CHOH^\\bullet\n\\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CHO}\n\\]\n\nthat is, formyl + 1-hydroxyethyl \u2192 lactaldehyde. Because radical\u2013radical recombination is barrierless once the partners meet, the chemistry is viable at extremely low temperature. Lactaldehyde was identified using isomer-selective photoionization mass spectrometry and isotopic substitution.\n\nThis experiment does **not make threonine**, but it solves an important upstream problem independently of terrestrial cyanosulfidic chemistry. Lactaldehyde made in molecular-cloud/protoplanetary ice could in principle be incorporated into asteroids or comets, undergo aqueous Strecker chemistry with HCN/NH\u2083 during parent-body alteration, or be delivered to an early planetary surface. Thus two conceptually different frameworks now converge on the same high-value network node, lactaldehyde:\n\n\\[\n\\text{surface HCN photochemistry} \\rightarrow \\text{lactaldehyde}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\text{CO/ethanol interstellar ice chemistry} \\rightarrow \\text{lactaldehyde}.\n\\]\n\n### Proposed complex-glycine-precursor route\n\n**Kuroda and Kobayashi (2024, *Molecules*)** proposed a broader \u201ccomplex glycine precursor\u201d or CGP framework in which energetic processing first creates a polymeric/complex glycine precursor and subsequent side-chain alkylation generates proteinogenic amino-acid skeletons. In their threonine scheme, **acetaldehyde is the proposed side-chain precursor**. The authors discuss hydrogen bonding as a possible source of preference for threonine over allothreonine.\n\nThis route must presently be kept separate from experimentally established threonine syntheses. The authors themselves identify kinetic and mechanistic uncertainties, and a complete CGP \u2192 threonine transformation with measured yield was not demonstrated. It is therefore useful as a **network hypothesis**, particularly because it again makes acetaldehyde a central C\u2082 precursor, but it should not be assigned the evidentiary weight of the Thanassi, Ivanov, Ritson\u2013Sutherland, Parker or ice experiments.\n\n## Intermediates, catalysts, and quantitative experimental evidence\n\nAcross otherwise very different frameworks, only a relatively small number of intermediates repeatedly matter.\n\n**Acetaldehyde (CH\u2083CHO)** is the most conspicuous hub. It supplies the methyl-bearing carbon unit in the aminomalononitrile reaction, reacts with HCN to produce lactonitrile in the cyanosulfidic route, reacts directly with glycine/oligoglycine in the Ivanov route, and is the proposed side-chain reagent in the CGP hypothesis. It also occurs naturally among products of energetic prebiotic chemistry.\n\n**Lactonitrile, CH\u2083CH(OH)CN**, is the key reduced-homologation intermediate linking acetaldehyde to lactaldehyde. **Lactaldehyde, CH\u2083CH(OH)CHO**, is then the direct carbonyl precursor required by a conventional Strecker construction of the threonine skeleton. The final immediate Strecker precursor is **2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile**, whose nitrile carbon becomes threonine's carboxyl carbon upon hydrolysis.\n\n**Aminomalononitrile, NH\u2082CH(CN)\u2082**, provides an alternative HCN-derived route in which the amino functionality and activated \u03b1-carbon are already present before acetaldehyde addition. This route bypasses lactaldehyde altogether.\n\n**Glycine** is the minimal amino-acid precursor in the Ivanov chemistry. Since glycine itself is among the easiest amino acids to obtain from discharge, HCN, Strecker and extraterrestrial chemistry, the glycine + acetaldehyde reaction could potentially act as a downstream convergence step rather than requiring a unique dedicated route from inorganic gases to threonine.\n\nA compact comparison of experimentally imposed conditions illustrates how different the routes actually are:\n\n| Study | Reaction conditions | Catalyst/energy | Threonine-specific outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Ritson & Sutherland 2013, Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.** | Representative photoreduction: ~10 mM nitrile, 30 mM H\u2082S, 33 mM phosphate, pH 7, 254 nm; CuCN ~10 mol%; 2 h | UV + H\u2082S; Cu(I) photoredox | Lactaldehyde **19%**; subsequent HCN/NH\u2083 gave Thr/allo-Thr aminonitrile. |\n| **Thanassi 1975, J. Mol. Evol.** | Mild, approximately neutral aqueous reaction of aminomalononitrile with acetaldehyde; subsequent acid hydrolysis/decarboxylation | **No mineral catalyst required** | D,L-threonine + allo-threonine; paper states products were quantified, but accessible abstract does not expose numeric yield. |\n| **Ivanov & Ivanov 1983, Origins of Life** | pH **3.3\u20133.7**, **60\u201380 \u00b0C**, glycine/oligoglycine + HCHO/CH\u2083CHO | Explicitly **no inorganic solids** | Threonine among several protein and nonprotein amino acids. |\n| **Sweeney, Toste & Ponnamperuma 1976, Origins of Life** | HCN **0.004\u20130.1 M**, ^60Co irradiation, about **200,000 rad h\u207b\u00b9** | \u03b3-radiation; no conventional catalyst | Thr among seven protein amino acids after hydrolysis. |\n| **Parker et al. 2011, PNAS** | CH\u2084/H\u2082S/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 \u2248 258/100/250/87 mmHg in Miller apparatus | Electrical spark; tungsten electrodes | Thr detected; absent from three comparison Miller sample sets. |\n| **Elsila et al. 2007, ApJ** | H\u2082O:C\u2082H\u2085OH:HCN:NH\u2083 = **20:2:1:1**, ~20 K | UV photolysis of ice | Hydrolysate: Gly = 100, Thr = **60**, allo-Thr = **30**. |\n| **Chen et al. 2008, MNRAS** | C\u2081\u2080H\u2088:H\u2082O:NH\u2083 = **1:1:1**, 15 K, 4\u201320 eV photons | UV/EUV | Thr relative abundance **13.6** vs Ala = 100; quantum yield **1.1\u00d710\u207b\u2076**. |\n| **Wang et al. 2024, Nat. Commun.** | CO/ethanol ice, **5 K**, electron irradiation | Energetic electrons/cosmic-ray proxy | Lactaldehyde, not Thr; barrierless HCO\u2022 + CH\u2083CHOH\u2022 recombination. |\n\nSeveral conclusions follow from the catalyst literature.\n\nThere is **no universal catalytic requirement** for threonine chemistry. Aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde and glycine/acetaldehyde reactions work without minerals; \u03b3-radiolysis and astrophysical-ice routes are energy-driven; discharge chemistry likewise does not require an intentionally added mineral.\n\nCopper has the clearest demonstrated catalytic role in the threonine-specific modern surface chemistry. CuCN/Cu(I) participates in photoredox cycling that accelerates nitrile reductions in the Ritson\u2013Sutherland system. However, H\u2082S-driven versions can function without Cu(I), and **sulfite/ferrocyanide** offers a distinct photoredox implementation of HCN reductive homologation. It would therefore be incorrect to encode \u201cCuCN required\u201d as an absolute dependency for every HCN-to-hydroxyaldehyde network.\n\nNo clay, FeS, NiS or alkaline-hydrothermal mineral has yet emerged from the threonine-specific literature as an experimentally established catalyst comparable to copper in the photoredox route. Generic amino-acid syntheses on minerals or in hydrothermal systems exist, but that is not equivalent to demonstrating threonine formation; the target-specific evidence surveyed here does not justify adding a \u201chydrothermal mineral \u2192 threonine\u201d edge merely by analogy.\n\nA final analytical caution is essential. **Strong acid hydrolysis is itself a chemical transformation.** In HCN-polymer, radiolysis, and several ice experiments, it converts nitriles, amides or oligomer-bound precursors into measurable amino acids. Such experiments establish that the irradiation or polymerization network contained the appropriate carbon\u2013nitrogen framework, but not necessarily that free threonine accumulated before laboratory hydrolysis. Dragani\u0107 et al.'s finding that irradiated cyanide gives oligomers that release amino acids is particularly explicit on this point.\n\n## Environmental contexts and geochemical plausibility\n\n### Sunlit surface waters, ponds, and evaporative basins\n\nOf the terrestrial environments, shallow surface waters are the closest fit to the Ritson\u2013Sutherland chemistry because the reactions need UV radiation, liquid water, cyanide and a reducing sulfur species. The difficult issue is not whether the organic reactions work but whether all necessary reagents can coexist at useful concentrations.\n\nThere is meaningful geochemical support for concentrating cyanide. **Jonathan Toner and David Catling (2019, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*)** modeled closed-basin alkaline lakes and experimentally constrained precipitation of **sodium ferrocyanide salts** over plausible early-Earth conditions. Evaporation and mineral weathering can stockpile atmospheric HCN as ferrocyanide; subsequent heating of sediments can release concentrated cyanide salts. This provides a concrete geological mechanism for overcoming at least part of the \u201cdilute HCN\u201d problem.\n\nThe sulfide side is more contentious. **Sukrit Ranjan, Zoe Todd, John Sutherland and Dimitar Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology*)** calculated early-Earth sulfide speciation and concluded that substantial aqueous HS\u207b concentrations are generally difficult to maintain because metal ions precipitate sulfides and because atmospheric sulfur species affect UV transmission. They explicitly identified a possible tension in cyanosulfidic scenarios: H\u2082S/SO\u2082 can provide sulfur-derived reducing species, but abundant sulfur gases/aerosols can also attenuate the UV needed to drive photochemistry. The authors did identify local geochemical circumstances\u2014and sulfite/bisulfite chemistry\u2014that can partly alleviate the problem, so this is a constraint rather than a disproof.\n\nAccordingly, a **localized, periodically concentrated surface setting** is better supported than a homogeneous \u201cprimordial ocean\u201d for this route. Evaporation, cyanide storage, water-level cycling and spatial separation could provide concentrations that open-ocean chemistry cannot. But no single field site or geochemical model has yet demonstrated simultaneous natural availability at laboratory-effective levels of HCN, ammonia, sulfur reductant, phosphate and photoredox catalyst.\n\n### Reducing atmospheres and volcanic environments\n\nThe Miller H\u2082S experiment establishes excellent **chemical possibility** but weaker global environmental plausibility. Modern discussions generally favor an early atmosphere substantially richer in N\u2082 and CO\u2082 than the highly reducing CH\u2084/NH\u2083 atmosphere used in classic Miller chemistry. **Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** nevertheless showed that even neutral N\u2082/CO\u2082 discharge mixtures can make significant amino acids if nitrite/nitrate-mediated destruction during workup is suppressed, for example by ferrous iron. Their result weakens the simplistic claim that atmospheric amino-acid synthesis requires a permanently methane-rich planet, although it did not specifically establish threonine production from neutral gas mixtures.\n\nFor threonine itself, the strongest discharge result remains Parker et al.'s H\u2082S-rich experiment, whose CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082S inventory is more plausibly treated as **local or transient**\u2014for example around reducing volcanic plumes\u2014than as a global atmospheric composition.\n\nImpact-generated reducing atmospheres provide another way to generate temporary H\u2082/CH\u2084/HCN-rich conditions. Modern atmospheric modeling finds that large impacts can produce reducing episodes lasting roughly **10\u2075\u201310\u2077 years**, with HCN production rising sharply when CH\u2084/CO\u2082 becomes sufficiently high. Such models do not synthesize threonine directly, but they provide a plausible upstream feedstock source for the HCN-dependent threonine routes.\n\nThus the atmospheric framework is best viewed as a **feedstock generator feeding aqueous chemistry**, rather than requiring threonine itself to be completed in the gas phase.\n\n### Warm acidic waters\n\nThe Ivanov glycine\u2013acetaldehyde reaction at pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C is chemically compatible with warm acidic pools, volcanic-lake margins or hydrothermally warmed surface waters. Unlike the cyanosulfidic pathway, it does not require UV or a metal catalyst during the threonine-forming step.\n\nIts environmental weakness is quantitative rather than qualitative: no geochemical model accompanying the original experiment showed that glycine, glycine oligomers and acetaldehyde would naturally reach the concentrations and residence times needed together. Since the reaction generated numerous amino acids rather than selectively accumulating threonine, concentrating and preserving the product is another unresolved problem.\n\n### Hydrothermal vents\n\nThe literature does **not** presently make high-temperature submarine hydrothermal vents the strongest threonine-specific setting. Although generic amino-acid syntheses have been investigated under hydrothermal conditions, target-specific evidence for efficient threonine synthesis is weak, while experimental degradation data point in the opposite direction.\n\nA 2005 *Chemistry Letters* experimental study, **\u201cThreonine Transformation under Hydrothermal Conditions,\u201d** heated threonine solutions between **121 and 160 \u00b0C** and observed conversion to glycine together with other products, including peptide/polymeric material. Thus temperatures characteristic of many vent reactors can destroy or transform threonine on geologically relevant residence times.\n\nThis does not exclude cooler hydrothermal gradients, mineral pores or low-temperature vent margins. It does mean that a network model should not simply equate \u201chydrothermal amino-acid synthesis\u201d with \u201cthreonine production\u201d; **formation and destruction must be modeled simultaneously**, and high-temperature exposure may act as a sink rather than a source for threonine.\n\n### Meteorite parent bodies and interstellar ices\n\nExtraterrestrial chemistry has unusually strong direct experimental support for assembling the threonine skeleton. The Elsila ethanol-ice experiment actually produces substantial threonine relative to glycine in the hydrolyzed residue, while Chen et al. independently produce threonine from PAH/H\u2082O/NH\u2083 ice. Wang et al. add a mechanistically clean route to lactaldehyde at 5 K.\n\nWhat is less secure is the transfer from laboratory ice residue to a meaningful early-Earth inventory. Irradiation doses, survival during ice warming and planetesimal processing, conversion of bound precursor into free amino acid, delivery rates and post-delivery destruction all have to be considered. Elsila et al.'s isotope experiments also caution against assigning simple textbook mechanisms to these residues: several pathways operate simultaneously.\n\nMeteorite observations do not currently provide unambiguous corroboration specifically for indigenous threonine. **Koga and Naraoka (2017, *Scientific Reports*)** detected many hydroxy amino acids in Murchison, but the observed L-enrichment of proteinogenic compounds\u2014including threonine\u2014coincided with proteinogenic amino acids in procedural blanks and was judged potentially attributable to terrestrial contamination. In their complementary aldehyde/NH\u2083 experiment, **threonine and allothreonine were not produced**, apart from a small amount of L-threonine interpreted as likely contamination.\n\nThat is an important negative constraint. The experiment used H\u2082O/NH\u2083/HCHO/CH\u2083CHO at a **1000:100:10:1 molar ratio**, with or without glycolaldehyde, heated at **60 \u00b0C for six days** under N\u2082. It successfully generated many other hydroxy amino acids, showing that \u201caldehydes + ammonia + aqueous parent-body alteration\u201d is not automatically sufficient to make threonine.\n\nConsequently, extraterrestrial threonine is chemically plausible and experimentally producible, but **meteorite occurrence by itself is presently poorer evidence for threonine than for many nonproteinogenic amino acids**, because terrestrial contamination is particularly difficult to exclude for a common biological amino acid.\n\n## Main unresolved questions and published critiques\n\n### The free-threonine versus precursor problem\n\nThis is probably the most important interpretive issue. The cyanosulfidic route very convincingly reaches the **threonine aminonitrile**, but a network designer must still assign kinetics to aminonitrile hydrolysis under realistic environmental pH and temperature. HCN-radiolysis and many ice experiments similarly reveal threonine mainly after laboratory hydrolysis.\n\nThe distinction matters because nitrile hydrolysis is irreversible enough to pull Strecker equilibria toward amino acids, but its uncatalyzed rate can be slow under mild neutral conditions. Strong analytical hydrolysis therefore cannot simply be represented as an instantaneous environmental reaction. A useful synthesis network should distinguish at least:\n\n\\[\n\\text{lactaldehyde}\n\\rightleftharpoons\n\\text{cyanohydrin}\n\\rightleftharpoons\n\\text{aminonitrile}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{threonine}\n\\]\n\nrather than collapsing all four species into one \u201cStrecker synthesis\u201d node. Ritson and Sutherland's observation that aminonitrile formation itself can require **days to weeks** reinforces the kinetic importance of intermediate persistence.\n\n### Cyanide concentration\n\nHCN is versatile but difficult to concentrate in bulk water. The strongest classic HCN-polymer pathways often operate at concentrations far above plausible unconcentrated rainfall or oceanic HCN. Toner and Catling's alkaline-lake/ferrocyanide model is therefore valuable because it provides a physically motivated concentration and storage mechanism, but it introduces its own mineralogical and thermal requirements.\n\nThe cyanosulfidic route is less extreme in substrate concentration than old bulk-HCN polymerization, but it still requires a setting able to supply cyanide repeatedly while preventing excessive loss through volatilization, hydrolysis, metal complexation or competing oligomerization.\n\n### Lactaldehyde availability\n\nFor a clean Strecker route, lactaldehyde is the central bottleneck. This was historically a weak point because hydroxy aldehydes are reactive and not obviously abundant geochemical species. The situation is now substantially better than it was a decade ago: Ritson and Sutherland produced lactaldehyde at **19% yield** by aqueous photoreduction, and Wang et al. independently demonstrated lactaldehyde production in irradiated CO/ethanol ices at 5 K.\n\nWhat remains unknown is whether either source creates environmentally persistent, sufficiently concentrated lactaldehyde before it undergoes oxidation, reduction, aldol chemistry, polymerization or other side reactions. This makes lactaldehyde an especially high-value target for future geochemical lifetime measurements.\n\n### Stereochemical selectivity\n\nNone of the experimentally mature abiotic routes solves the biological stereochemistry problem. Thanassi explicitly obtained **D,L-threonine and allo-threonine**; the Elsila ice experiment produced both D,L-threonine and D,L-allothreonine, at relative abundances 60 and 30 respectively; radiolysis experiments gave approximately racemic chiral amino acids.\n\nThus the problem is actually two-dimensional: enrichment of **threonine versus allothreonine** and enrichment of **L versus D** within the threonine pair. No demonstrated prebiotic synthesis surveyed here simultaneously delivers strong threo diastereoselectivity and L enantioselectivity. Claims that one route is especially \u201cbiological\u201d because it makes proteinogenic threonine should therefore be treated cautiously unless the stereoisomer distribution is reported.\n\n### Product complexity and selectivity\n\nThe cleanest-looking reaction schemes do not necessarily yield clean mixtures. Electrical discharge gives dozens of amino acids and amines; the glycine/aldehyde route gives many protein and nonprotein amino acids; ice irradiation gives a chemically complex organic residue; HCN polymerization generates heterogeneous nitrogen-rich solids and oligomers.\n\nThis matters for origin-of-life network design because **absolute yield is not enough**. Competition for acetaldehyde, HCN, ammonia and reducing equivalents may suppress threonine under a fully connected reaction network even when a binary or ternary model reaction works. Conversely, promiscuous networks may be beneficial if the objective is simultaneous access to many building blocks rather than selective threonine manufacture.\n\n### Compatibility of UV and sulfur chemistry\n\nThis is the most explicit geochemical critique of the cyanosulfidic framework. Ranjan et al. found that aqueous hydrosulfide is difficult to sustain generically because Fe and Cu remove sulfide as minerals, while sulfur-rich atmospheres and aerosols can shield the short-wave UV required by photochemistry. They also showed that sulfite and bisulfite can be more readily supplied in some surface waters, and Xu et al. experimentally demonstrated useful photoredox chemistry with sulfite/ferrocyanide. The route is therefore **contested in environmental implementation, not in laboratory chemistry**.\n\nAn important consequence is that \u201csulfidic surface pond\u201d should not be modeled as a single unconstrained reservoir. Metal sulfide precipitation, sulfur atmospheric opacity, cyanide-metal complexation and water depth all alter the simultaneous availability of light and reductant.\n\n### Atmospheric composition\n\nParker et al.'s H\u2082S experiment is analytically convincing but geochemically nonunique. A global atmosphere with the experiment's very high CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082S partial pressures is not required\u2014and is difficult to reconcile with many atmosphere models. Neutral-atmosphere discharge experiments demonstrate that organics can still be generated, but the threonine-specific result has not been reproduced at comparable strength under a fully CO\u2082/N\u2082-dominated mixture.\n\nThe defensible interpretation is therefore that discharge chemistry remains viable especially in **local reducing plumes, transient impact-generated atmospheres, or other disequilibrium settings**, while a globally persistent Miller-type atmosphere is not necessary and should not be assumed.\n\n### High-temperature survival\n\nA proposed pathway must produce threonine faster than the environment destroys it. Direct hydrothermal experiments show threonine transforming at **121\u2013160 \u00b0C**, including formation of glycine and other products. This makes hot hydrothermal residence a potentially serious sink.\n\nConsequently, a hydrothermal scenario would be more credible if threonine forms in cooler peripheral regions or is rapidly exported down a thermal gradient. Direct synthesis and decomposition kinetics need to be measured in the same mineral/fluid compositions before high-temperature vents can be ranked alongside surface or extraterrestrial routes.\n\n### The formose/ammonia result argues against assuming \u201cgeneric aldehydes make Thr\u201d\n\nKoga and Naraoka's 2017 experiment is particularly valuable because it falsifies an overly broad assumption. Their aldehyde + NH\u2083 system generated an impressive spectrum of C\u2083/C\u2084 hydroxy amino acids, yet **not threonine or allothreonine** at convincing indigenous levels.\n\nThis means that carbon-counting alone is insufficient. Threonine requires the correct positional relationship:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HO{-}CH(CH_3){-}CH(NH_2){-}COOH}.\n\\]\n\nMany formose/aldol pathways favor alternative \u03b1-, \u03b2- and \u03b3-hydroxy amino-acid skeletons instead. In network terms, the route from generic HCHO/CH\u2083CHO/NH\u2083 chemistry to C\u2084H\u2089NO\u2083 is **not** equivalent to a route to threonine.\n\n### Congruence with biology is currently weak\n\nThe experimentally demonstrated prebiotic routes surveyed here use HCN oligomerization, Strecker aminonitriles, aldehyde\u2013glycine condensation, photoredox homologation, discharge chemistry or radical ice chemistry. None gains strong evidentiary support merely by resembling modern cellular threonine synthesis. The absence of biochemical congruence is not a disproof\u2014modern enzymatic metabolism need not preserve the primordial synthetic route\u2014but it means \u201ccontinuity with extant metabolism\u201d currently provides little leverage for choosing among these threonine scenarios.\n\nA more productive comparison is at the **network-node level**: acetaldehyde, glycine, cyanide, hydroxy aldehydes and amino nitriles are products or substrates shared across several prebiotic frameworks. That convergence is experimentally better supported than any claim that one complete pathway is uniquely ancestral.\n\n## Comparative judgment for synthesis-network design\n\nFor a reaction-network model, the literature supports treating the following edges as experimentally grounded:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{acetaldehyde + HCN \\rightarrow lactonitrile}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{lactonitrile \\xrightarrow[H_2S]{UV,\\;Cu(I)} lactaldehyde}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{lactaldehyde + HCN + NH_3 \\rightarrow Thr/alloThr\\ aminonitrile}}\n\\]\n\nwith the lactaldehyde-forming step reported at **19%** under the Ritson\u2013Sutherland photoredox regime.\n\nA second independently grounded branch is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{HCN \\rightarrow aminomalononitrile}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde\n\\rightarrow hydrolyzable\\ Thr/alloThr\\ precursor}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{hydrolysis + decarboxylation\\rightarrow Thr/alloThr}}\n\\]\n\nwith no mineral catalyst needed for the acetaldehyde addition.\n\nA third is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde\n\\rightarrow Thr + other\\ amino\\ acids}}\n\\]\n\nat pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C without inorganic solids.\n\nThe radiation, discharge and ice experiments should initially be represented as **network-generating reaction ensembles** rather than single elementary reactions because the threonine-forming sequence has not been resolved. Their experimental output constraints are nevertheless useful: threonine is definitely accessible under those conditions.\n\nOn current evidence, the most defensible environmental ranking is not a single winner:\n\n**For mechanistic definition**, the aqueous cyanosulfidic/lactaldehyde route is strongest because individual intermediates and a lactaldehyde yield are known.\n\n**For chemical simplicity of the target-specific C\u2013C-forming step**, aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde and glycine + acetaldehyde are particularly attractive because they do not require a mineral catalyst.\n\n**For direct experimental production from very simple energetic starting mixtures**, the H\u2082S electrical-discharge experiment is compelling, but the environmental gas composition is contested.\n\n**For exogenous synthesis**, the ethanol/HCN/NH\u2083 ice experiment provides unusually strong relative threonine production, and the independent 2024 lactaldehyde experiment supplies a plausible upstream Strecker precursor. The main uncertainties shift from synthesis to hydrolysis, survival and delivery.\n\n**For hot hydrothermal synthesis**, the threonine-specific case is currently weakest: direct synthesis evidence is limited and measured thermal transformation provides a clear degradation constraint.\n\nThe single most important experimental gaps are therefore quantitative rather than conceptual: threonine/allo-threonine yields for the aminomalononitrile and glycine\u2013acetaldehyde routes under rigorously prebiotic workups; direct aminonitrile-to-free-threonine kinetics at environmentally realistic pH, temperature and ionic strength; lactaldehyde lifetimes in mixed geochemical solutions; effects of minerals on both formation and destruction; absolute rather than relative yields in ice experiments; and full stereoisomer-resolved product distributions. These measurements would determine which of the many chemically possible pathways can carry appreciable flux in an integrated prebiotic reaction network.\n\n## Primary literature cited\n\n**Ritson, D. J. & Sutherland, J. D. (2013), *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*, \u201cSynthesis of Aldehydic Ribonucleotide and Amino Acid Precursors by Photoredox Chemistry.\u201d** Demonstrated HCN-derived photoreductive homologation, 19% lactaldehyde from the acetaldehyde-cyanohydrin system, and conversion to the (allo-)threonine Strecker aminonitrile.\n\n**Patel, B. H.; Percivalle, C.; Ritson, D. J.; Duffy, C. D.; Sutherland, J. D. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, \u201cCommon origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism.\u201d** Embedded threonine-precursor chemistry in a common HCN/H\u2082S/UV reaction network.\n\n**Xu, J.; Ritson, D. J.; Ranjan, S.; Todd, Z. R.; Sasselov, D. D.; Sutherland, J. D. (2018), *Chemical Communications*, \u201cPhotochemical reductive homologation of hydrogen cyanide using sulfite and ferrocyanide.\u201d** Demonstrated an alternative sulfite/ferrocyanide photoredox implementation of HCN homologation.\n\n**Thanassi, J. W. (1975), *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 7, 65\u201373, \u201cReactions of aminomalononitrile with electrophiles.\u201d** Direct experimental source for D,L-threonine and allo-threonine from aminomalononitrile/aldehyde chemistry after hydrolysis.\n\n**Ivanov, C. P. & Ivanov, O. C. (1983), *Origins of Life* 13, 97\u2013108, \u201cA study of the interaction of glycine and its oligohomopeptides with formaldehyde and acetaldehyde under possible primitive earth conditions.\u201d** Demonstrated threonine formation at pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C without inorganic solids.\n\n**Lowe, C. U.; Rees, M. W.; Markham, R. (1963), *Nature* 199, 219\u2013222, \u201cSynthesis of Complex Organic Compounds from Simple Precursors: Formation of Amino-Acids, Amino-Acid Polymers, Fatty Acids and Purines from Ammonium Cyanide.\u201d** Foundational bulk-ammonium-cyanide chemistry.\n\n**Matthews, C. N. & Moser, R. E. (1967), *Nature* 215, 1230\u20131234, \u201cPeptide Synthesis from Hydrogen Cyanide and Water.\u201d** Demonstrated that HCN polymers yield peptide/amino-acid material upon reaction with water; closely related experiments identified threonine in hydrolysates.\n\n**Sweeney, M. A.; Toste, A. P.; Ponnamperuma, C. (1976), *Origins of Life* 7, 187\u2013189, \u201cFormation of amino acids by cobalt-60 irradiation of hydrogen cyanide solutions.\u201d** Demonstrated threonine after \u03b3-irradiation of 0.004\u20130.1 M HCN and hydrolysis.\n\n**Dragani\u0107, Z.; Dragani\u0107, I.; Shimoyama, A.; Ponnamperuma, C. (1977), *Origins of Life* 8, 371\u2013376, \u201cEvidence for amino acids in hydrolysates of compounds formed by ionizing radiations. I. Aqueous solutions of HCN, NH\u2084CN, and NaCN.\u201d** Established radical-initiated cyanide oligomer formation, amino-acid release by hydrolysis and near-racemic products.\n\n**Parker, E. T.; Cleaves, H. J.; Dworkin, J. P.; Glavin, D. P.; Callahan, M.; Aubrey, A.; Lazcano, A.; Bada, J. L.; Joyce, G. F. (2011), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* 108, 5526\u20135531, \u201cPrimordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H\u2082S-rich spark discharge experiment.\u201d** Direct threonine detection from H\u2082S/CH\u2084/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 spark chemistry.\n\n**Elsila, J. E.; Dworkin, J. P.; Bernstein, M. P.; Martin, M. P.; Sandford, S. A. (2007), *The Astrophysical Journal* 660, 911\u2013918, \u201cMechanisms of Amino Acid Formation in Interstellar Ice Analogs.\u201d** Ethanol-containing UV ice yielded D,L-threonine = 60 and D,L-allothreonine = 30 relative to glycine = 100.\n\n**Chen, Y.-J.; Nuevo, M.; Yih, T.-S.; Ip, W.-H.; Fung, H.-S.; Cheng, C.-Y.; Tsai, H.-R.; Wu, C.-Y. R. (2008), *Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society* 384, 605\u2013610, \u201cAmino acids produced from the ultraviolet/extreme-ultraviolet irradiation of naphthalene in a H\u2082O+NH\u2083 ice mixture.\u201d** Threonine relative abundance 13.6 versus alanine = 100 and quantum yield \\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\).\n\n**Wang et al. (2024), *Nature Communications* 15, \u201cInterstellar formation of lactaldehyde, a key intermediate in the methylglyoxal pathway.\u201d** Directly demonstrated lactaldehyde formation by HCO\u2022 + CH\u2083CHOH\u2022 radical recombination in CO/ethanol ice at 5 K.\n\n**Koga, T. & Naraoka, H. (2017), *Scientific Reports* 7, \u201cA new family of extraterrestrial amino acids in the Murchison meteorite.\u201d** Critical negative result: aldehyde/NH\u2083 experiments produced many hydroxy amino acids but not convincing threonine/allo-threonine; meteoritic proteinogenic threonine was vulnerable to a terrestrial-contamination interpretation.\n\n**Koga et al. (2021), *Meteoritics & Planetary Science* 56, \u201cExtraterrestrial hydroxy amino acids in CM and CR carbonaceous chondrites.\u201d** Extended the meteoritic hydroxy-amino-acid inventory and formation analysis.\n\n**Toner, J. D. & Catling, D. C. (2019), *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 260, 124\u2013132, \u201cAlkaline lake settings for concentrated prebiotic cyanide and the origin of life.\u201d** Provides a geochemical mechanism for cyanide concentration/storage through ferrocyanide precipitation in closed-basin alkaline lakes.\n\n**Ranjan, S.; Todd, Z. R.; Sutherland, J. D.; Sasselov, D. D. (2018), *Astrobiology*, \u201cSulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry.\u201d** Quantified the difficulties of sustaining high HS\u207b and the tension between sulfur-rich environments and UV-dependent synthesis while identifying sulfite/bisulfite alternatives.\n\n**Cleaves, H. J.; Chalmers, J. H.; Lazcano, A.; Miller, S. L.; Bada, J. L. (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, \u201cA reassessment of prebiotic organic synthesis in neutral planetary atmospheres.\u201d** Demonstrated substantial discharge amino-acid production from more neutral atmospheric mixtures when oxidative workup losses are controlled.\n\n**Kuroda et al. (2020), *Natural Product Communications*, \u201cReaction of Aminomalononitrile and Benzylic Compounds as a Plausible Route to Phenylalanine.\u201d** Modern experimental treatment of the aminomalononitrile framework and explicit discussion of Thanassi's near-neutral acetaldehyde \u2192 threonine chemistry.\n\n**Kuroda & Kobayashi (2024), *Molecules* 29, 4403, \u201cAlkylation of Complex Glycine Precursor (CGP) as a Prebiotic Route to 20 Proteinogenic Amino Acids Synthesis.\u201d** Proposed, rather than demonstrated, an acetaldehyde/CGP route to threonine.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.8\n- Feedback: The network is structurally valid: the molecule and reaction inventory is sufficient, IDs are used consistently, all nodes belong to one connected graph, and directed routes reach the declared DL-threonine target. The deterministic balance check is accepted as ground truth: all 17 reactions are balanced or balanceable, with no conservation violations. Its coefficients do not establish yields, mechanisms or stereoisomer ratios. Literature alignment is strong at the reaction-class level, and the network responsibly labels substrate extrapolations, the diglycine proxy and unresolved ensembles. Its racemic target definition and separate allothreonine representation are appropriate. The main limitation is operational completeness: reaction records provide only a broad Surface designation rather than explicit conditions, catalyst inventories, residence times and material-handling scenarios. Several branches still rely on laboratory hydrolysis to demonstrate the target, and glycine-dependent chemistry requires integration with upstream glycine production. Reaction-level citations and quantitative formation-versus-destruction evidence would materially strengthen the assessment. Flagged reactions indicate evidentiary or implementation gaps, not atom-balance failures. This is a credible literature-grounded synthesis network, but it does not yet demonstrate environmentally sustained free-threonine production and does not meet the 0.90 network approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['This is a coherent implementation variant, not an independently established threonine mechanism. The supplied literature supports copper-free sulfide photochemistry in the broader network, but does not establish equivalent lactonitrile-to-lactaldehyde performance for rxn_003. The network appropriately labels this extrapolation. Removing copper does not by itself resolve sulfide availability, UV attenuation or the final hydrolysis bottleneck.', 'Xu et al. (2018) support sulfite-ferrocyanide reductive homologation, but the supplied evidence does not demonstrate this specific lactonitrile-to-threonine sequence. The proposed net reduction is conservation-valid according to the supplied check. Ferrocyanide is identified in the reaction name, but its concentration, availability and operating conditions are not specified. The network correctly treats this branch as an extrapolation rather than a demonstrated threonine endpoint.', 'The supplied literature supports oligoglycine-aldehyde chemistry, but not necessarily the exact glycylglycine-plus-acetaldehyde transformation and product release represented here. The net equation is conservation-valid, and the network appropriately identifies diglycine as a provisional proxy. The unresolved issues are substrate specificity, the site and sequence of modification, and peptide hydrolysis or amino-acid release under the stated conditions.', 'Historical HCN/ammonia experiments, including Matthews and Moser, support threonine detection after hydrolysis of complex reaction products. Representing this as an ensemble is appropriate and avoids inventing an elementary mechanism. Nevertheless, high cyanide concentrations and strong-acid hydrolysis are substantial implementation requirements. The balance-check coefficients establish conservation only; they do not establish the actual product distribution or measured ensemble stoichiometry.', 'Sweeney et al. (1976) and Dragani\u0107 et al. (1977) support threonine recovery after irradiation and hydrolysis of aqueous cyanide products. The ensemble representation is scientifically appropriate. The main environmental limitation is the unspecified natural radiation source and dose budget: the cited laboratory dose rate of approximately 2 kGy per hour cannot simply be assigned to an ordinary surface pool. Hydrolytic release and survival during continued irradiation also require specification.', 'Parker et al. (2011) provide strong product-level evidence that an H2S-rich wet discharge ensemble can yield threonine. The network correctly avoids assigning an unresolved elementary pathway. Environmental confidence is lower because the experimentally reducing gas mixture, discharge exposure and aqueous collection process are not instantiated in the submitted surface environment. Possible electrode/sulfur effects also limit direct translation to natural lightning. The listed sulfur and hydrogen products should be treated as formal net bookkeeping unless quantitatively supported.', 'The historical dry methane-ammonia discharge work supports hydrolyzable residues that release threonine. Water in the combined net equation is not inconsistent with a dry discharge because the pathway explicitly assigns hydrolysis to a later stage. However, the natural production and collection of the dry residue, its subsequent wetting, and the conditions required to release free threonine are unspecified. The evidence therefore supports target-bearing precursor material more strongly than environmental accumulation of free threonine.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.8\n- Feedback: This is a connected, chemically credible synthesis-network map with consistent molecule IDs, sufficient nodes and routes reaching the racemic target. The deterministic balance check is accepted: eight reactions are balanced as listed, ten are balanceable with coefficients, and none violates atom or charge conservation. The inferred coefficients neither establish product yields nor impose measured threonine/allothreonine ratios. Problematic-reaction listings identify evidentiary or operating-condition gaps, not conservation failures. Literature alignment is strong: the network distinguishes demonstrated precursors from recovered amino acids, represents unresolved chemistry as ensembles, and does not claim ten independently demonstrated mechanisms. Racemic synthesis and allothreonine coproduct formation are compatible with the stated target. Reaction-level citations should nevertheless be attached to the supplied node records. The main limitation is environmental specification and integration. The reaction list provides Surface labels but does not assign quantitative pH, temperature, residence time, catalyst availability or energy exposure to its stages. Literature benchmarks are not automatically network operating conditions. A staged surface setting is permissible, but collection, condition changes and intermediate survival must be physically documented. Environmentally effective hydrolysis is a recurring unresolved dependency; sulfide/UV/Cu compatibility, radiation dosimetry, reducing discharge mixtures and reactions within crude product mixtures also need testing. Acknowledging these limitations is appropriate but does not resolve them. P2 has the strongest combination of identified chemistry and direct target evidence. Overall, the network supports laboratory chemical accessibility more strongly than completed environmental synthesis. It is not approved at the network threshold of 0.90; individual pathway flags use the separately requested 0.80 threshold.\n- Issues: ['HCN-derived material can release glycine on hydrolysis, and the glycine\u2013acetaldehyde transformation has separate experimental support. Representing the upstream chemistry as an unresolved ensemble is appropriate. Nevertheless, analytical recovery of glycine does not establish its availability under the proposed surface conditions. This is a distinct graph route from P7, but threonine already recovered from rxn_015 cannot also be treated as evidence for additional synthesis through rxn_014.', 'Sweeney et al. (1976) and Dragani\u0107 et al. (1977) support amino-acid recovery, including glycine, after cyanide irradiation and hydrolysis. Coupling recovered glycine to the Ivanov reaction is chemically coherent but has not been demonstrated as an integrated environmental sequence. The laboratory radiation dose rate, approximately 2 kGy per hour in the cited experiment, requires a natural absorbed-dose justification. Hydrolytic release and irradiation-induced destruction remain additional limitations.', 'The Parker et al. (2011) discharge experiment supports an amino-acid inventory containing glycine and threonine. Using its glycine output in a separately supported acetaldehyde reaction is a legitimate network route. However, compatibility of the crude collected material with that downstream reaction is untested. Local reducing gas composition, collection efficiency, workup and possible tungsten/sulfur electrode effects remain unresolved. Direct threonine recovery from the discharge ensemble belongs to P9 rather than demonstrating this glycine-mediated sequence.', 'The Matthews and Moser studies support threonine recovery from hydrolyzed HCN-derived material. The ensemble representation correctly avoids inventing a molecular formula for heterogeneous polymers. Its balanced net equation is bookkeeping, not a measured product distribution or resolved mechanism. The central environmental gap is conversion of bound precursors into free threonine: strong laboratory acid hydrolysis establishes chemical accessibility but does not establish release in a surface pool.', 'Independent cyanide-radiolysis studies support post-hydrolysis threonine detection, so this is an experimentally grounded ensemble rather than a speculative carbon-counting route. Its use as a terrestrial synthesis pathway nevertheless requires both a realistic radiation exposure and an effective hydrolytic release process. The network acknowledges these gaps but supplies neither a dose budget nor operating conditions that establish environmental completion.', 'The supplied Matthews and Moser evidence supports threonine release after hydrolysis of residues generated by dry methane/ammonia discharge. Water in the net equation is consistent with the explicitly subsequent aqueous workup and is not a contradiction of the dry irradiation stage. However, the natural dry-gas reaction setting, residue collection and effective hydrolytic release remain unspecified. The balanced equation does not establish the detailed precursor chemistry or measured hydrogen evolution.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct a connected, surface-based network with 22 molecule nodes, 17 reaction nodes and 10 graph-distinct pathways. The target is racemic threonine, the (2R,3S)/(2S,3R) pair, not enantiopure L-threonine; allothreonine is represented separately. The best-resolved branches are lactaldehyde/Strecker chemistry, aminomalononitrile/acetaldehyde chemistry and glycine/acetaldehyde chemistry. Four additional reaction nodes represent experimentally supported but molecularly unresolved reaction ensembles. Three pathways connect glycine recovered from those ensembles to the separately demonstrated glycine/acetaldehyde reaction. These are ten distinct network routes, not ten independently demonstrated threonine mechanisms. Cyanide-rich preparation, near-neutral photochemistry and acidic hydrolysis occur in separate staged reservoirs; no single pond is assumed to support incompatible conditions simultaneously. Numerical conditions explicitly identified as proposed are test conditions, not reported experimental results or established natural operating conditions. Strong-acid analytical recovery is distinguished from proposed environmental release throughout. Consequently, this is a complete candidate reaction graph, not a demonstration that ten environmental processes sustain free-threonine production. Natural radiation dose, discharge energy, intermediate transfer, hydrolysis rates and formation-versus-destruction kinetics remain unresolved where stated. Unresolved ensembles are represented by staged net reaction nodes rather than fictitious polymer molecules. Their CO2, H2, NH3 and sulfur outputs provide formal conservation closure, not measured product distributions. Inferred coefficients do not establish yields or stereoisomer ratios. Neutral parent formulas are used for pH-dependent species; protonation and spectator counterions are handled in conditions. Null environment_formed identifies externally supplied feedstocks, including species also regenerated internally. Coproducts use the permitted intermediate role. No molecular oxygen, unjustified mineral catalyst, cryogenic extraterrestrial step or hot-vent threonine-production step is introduced.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H5NO/c1-3(5)2-4/h3,5H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactaldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine-family aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine-family aminoamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"rac-(2R,3S)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-threonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"rac-(2R,3R)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-allothreonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminomalononitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminomalononitrile-acetaldehyde addition product\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, near-neutral receiving pool; proposed pH 7-8 and 10-30 mM total concentrations of each substrate. Sample over 0.5-24 h rather than assuming quantitative conversion. These concentration and residence-time ranges are design assumptions, not a quoted lactonitrile preparation. Volatile feedstocks require continuing supply or demonstrable retention; equilibrium includes free acetaldehyde, HCN and their hydrated or ionized forms.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the acetaldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer forms racemic lactonitrile reversibly.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the chemically identified entry to the threonine-specific reductive homologation branch in Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). No mineral is required for cyanohydrin formation. Simultaneous aldehyde and cyanide availability, rather than carbon counting alone, is necessary. The pool formulation above remains an operating proposal.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted sulfide photoreduction of lactonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"CuCN-derived Cu(I)-cyanide complexes, photoredox catalyst\",\n        \"Phosphate buffer, pH-control agent rather than catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed temperature-controlled photochemical stage\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Benchmark activation: 254 nm irradiation for 2 h. Proposed starting formulation: 10 mM lactonitrile, 30 mM H2S, 33 mM phosphate, nominal 1 mM CuCN and pH 7. These concentrations are transferred from the representative glycolonitrile experiment, not claimed as the exact reported lactonitrile recipe. Use a shallow optical path and measure transmitted UV and absorbed fluence; two laboratory hours cannot be equated to two hours of sunlight. Allow sulfur-rich solids to settle before downstream processing, while measuring dissolved copper, sulfide and organic recovery.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox reduction of the nitrile through an imine-level intermediate, followed by hydrolysis, yields lactaldehyde and ammonia. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. Elemental sulfur represents a possible terminal oxidized-sulfur product for net electron balance; actual sulfur and polysulfide speciation is unresolved here.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) reported 19% lactaldehyde after 2 h and subsequent access to the threonine-family aminonitrile. The 42% glycolaldehyde result from their representative formulation is not assigned to lactaldehyde. Copper is included because it is supported in this implementation, not because every sulfide photoreduction requires it. Ranjan et al. (2018) identify sulfide precipitation and sulfur-related UV attenuation as major deployment constraints. Fe2+-rich water therefore cannot be assumed to preserve the nominal dissolved sulfide and copper concentrations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7-8.5 with additional cyanide supplied to the cooled lactaldehyde-containing photolysate. A 0.5-2 day sampling window is proposed, not reported as a completion time. Transfer promptly after rxn_002; measure aldehyde loss during settling, transport and cyanide addition. No purification or quantitative recovery of lactaldehyde is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde gives 2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile, with multiple stereoisomers possible.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) describe lactaldehyde conversion through its cyanohydrin before aminonitrile formation. Explicitly retaining this species avoids treating the entire Strecker sequence as instantaneous. Lactaldehyde hydration, competing condensation and persistence in crude photolysate remain unquantified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Excess ammonia in a separate, UV-shielded aqueous stage. Proposed pH 8-9 and 50-200 mM total ammonia; monitor over 7-28 days. The ammonia concentration is a design range, whereas days-to-weeks aminonitrile formation is supported at the reaction-class level. Account for ammonia and HCN volatilization and competing cyanide chemistry. Do not begin acidic hydrolysis until the aminonitrile inventory is measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin re-equilibration exposes lactaldehyde; ammonia forms an imine or iminium species, which cyanide captures. The net equation is not direct nucleophilic displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) demonstrated conversion to the threonine/allothreonine Strecker aminonitrile using HCN and excess ammonia. Their work supports the precursor, not an isolated free-threonine yield. This slow stage is a kinetic bottleneck, and neither threo selectivity nor L enrichment is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of the threonine-family aminonitrile to aminoamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by an HCl-bearing acidic surface reservoir\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed environmental hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed test point: pH 1.5 and 363 K, sampled over 1-30 days. These conditions are not demonstrated completion conditions for this substrate. Transfer from rxn_004 requires a measured acid-equivalent budget to overcome ammonia and mineral buffering. Use a separate acidic reservoir; no instantaneous pH switch in one pond is assumed. Strong-HCl hydrolysis near 373 K is a laboratory comparator, not an environmental step silently substituted here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted nitrile hydration produces the corresponding aminoamide; protonated species dominate under these conditions but are represented by neutral parent formulas.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically justified but environmentally unvalidated extension beyond the precursor endpoint of Ritson and Sutherland (2013). The aminoamide is a mechanistically expected intermediate, not a separately isolated product claimed from that study. Substrate-specific hydration rates under the proposed conditions are missing, so this edge carries unknown environmental flux.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoamide hydrolysis and release of threonine-family amino acids\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium in the acidic hydrolysis reservoir\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K during proposed hydrolysis; 300-310 K after collection\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue the pH 1.5, 363 K candidate time course from rxn_005, with separate quantification of nitrile, amide, threonine and allothreonine. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium. Cool collected aliquots promptly. A 30-day observation window is proposed, not a claim of conversion. Determine net accumulation from formation and loss measured in the same fluid; do not assume temperatures below 394 K eliminate degradation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the carboxamide releases ammonia and the corresponding amino acid. The stereoisomeric precursor pool can yield both racemic threonine and racemic allothreonine; no fixed ratio is imposed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"General amide hydrolysis supports the transformation, but the supplied threonine-specific photoredox studies do not establish its environmental rate or free-product yield. This is therefore an explicitly unvalidated completion step relative to Ritson and Sutherland (2013). The reported hydrothermal transformation of threonine at 394-433 K, in 'Threonine Transformation under Hydrothermal Conditions' (2005), motivates avoiding hot vents but does not supply a safe-temperature threshold or a low-temperature destruction rate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN oligomerization to aminomalononitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ammonia/cyanide basic medium, promoting HCN oligomerization\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"general-base promotion by the ammonia/cyanide medium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed concentrated alkaline preparation: 0.1-1 M total cyanide, pH 8-9, sampled from 6 h to 7 days. These are screening conditions, not a reported selective aminomalononitrile yield. Capture or transfer an aminomalononitrile-containing fraction before extensive higher oligomerization. A selective natural separation is not assumed. Cyanide concentration and retention must be established independently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise HCN oligomerization accesses its trimer, aminomalononitrile, within a competing oligomer network.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminomalononitrile is the HCN trimer used by Thanassi (1975); the broader competing HCN oligomer chemistry is supported by Lowe et al. (1963) and Matthews and Moser (1967). These sources do not establish a selective environmental HCN-to-trimer process at the proposed conditions. Toner and Catling (2019) provide a cyanide concentration framework, not a guarantee of simultaneous trimer accumulation and acetaldehyde supply.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-carbon addition of aminomalononitrile to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed ambient-temperature implementation\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately neutral water, proposed pH 6.5-7.5. Test 5-20 mM aminomalononitrile and comparable acetaldehyde over 0.5-7 days. Concentrations and residence times are proposed because the supplied summary does not provide the original values. Adjust the alkaline feed from rxn_007 before aldehyde addition and quantify trimer loss and competing oligomers during that adjustment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reaction at the activated central carbon of aminomalononitrile forms a C-C bond to acetaldehyde, generating the hydroxyethyl-substituted dinitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thanassi (1975) directly supports acetaldehyde/aminomalononitrile chemistry yielding DL-threonine and allothreonine after hydrolysis. Kuroda et al. (2020) describe the C-C-forming stage as nearly neutral aqueous chemistry. No mineral catalyst is justified. The defined adduct is a mechanistic representation of the productive branch, not an assertion that the crude mixture contains only this species.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of the substituted aminomalononitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium in an HCl-bearing acidic reservoir\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed environmental test window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-2, with a test point of pH 1.5 and 363 K over 1-30 days. Quantify both nitrile groups, intermediate amides and released ammonia. The supplied literature summary establishes laboratory acid hydrolysis but does not give the exact original acid concentration and residence time. Those missing values are not invented or equated with this candidate environmental stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential hydration and hydrolysis of both nitrile groups gives a substituted aminomalonic acid and ammonia. The dicarboxylic acid may be transient because decarboxylation can accompany hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acid hydrolysis followed by decarboxylation is supported as the product-releasing sequence by Thanassi (1975) and its discussion in Kuroda et al. (2020). The separate dicarboxylic-acid node resolves the mechanism but is not claimed as an isolated intermediate in those experiments. Completion at pH 1-2 and 350-370 K remains unvalidated; strong laboratory hydrolysis cannot establish its environmental rate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Decarboxylation of hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K during release; cool promptly afterward\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic aqueous conditions inherited from rxn_009, proposed pH 1-3. Monitor decarboxylation during the same 1-30 day test rather than assigning instantaneous conversion. Measure threonine/allothreonine formation and loss independently. CO2 removal may favor net decarboxylation, but neither a removal rate nor a product yield is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of one carboxyl group from the substituted aminomalonic acid produces the alpha-amino-acid skeleton. Protonation generates stereoisomeric threonine-family products.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thanassi (1975) demonstrated DL-threonine and allothreonine after the overall hydrolysis/decarboxylation sequence. This supplies stronger product-level evidence than the photoredox precursor endpoint, but the supplied research does not resolve the decarboxylation rate under the proposed environmental conditions. No numerical yield or stereoisomer ratio is inferred.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed upstream glycine preparation\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8-9, 10-30 mM formaldehyde and total cyanide, and 50-200 mM total ammonia. Sample over 1-14 days. These values define a testable module, not a protocol supplied by the threonine papers. Monitor formaldehyde sequestration into competing ammonia adducts and cyanohydrins. Use a separate cyanide-rich stage before acidic hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia form imine-level species that undergo cyanide addition to aminoacetonitrile; cyanohydrin equilibria can participate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker chemistry is a standard chemically justified route to the glycine skeleton. Nevertheless, this particular operating module and its integration with threonine production are speculative relative to the supplied evidence: Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015) support related aminonitrile chemistry, not the exact proposed glycine preparation. The edge is included to make the glycine-dependent branch explicit rather than importing glycine as an unexplained complex feedstock.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium in an acidic hydrolysis reservoir\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed environmental release window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-2 and 1-30 day observation window, with pH 1.5 and 363 K as a test point. Measure glycine directly before any stronger analytical hydrolysis. For transfer to rxn_013, cool, determine remaining cyanide and ammonia, and adjust the measured solution to pH 3.3-3.7. Required dilution and resulting glycine concentration must be recorded.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydration followed by amide hydrolysis releases glycine and ammonia; the transient glycinamide is not separately inventoried.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is standard nitrile-to-amino-acid chemistry, but the supplied literature does not validate the proposed environmental time course. It is an explicitly unvalidated integration step, not evidence that glycine recovered by strong analytical hydrolysis was already freely available. Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) support the subsequent glycine/aldehyde chemistry, not this upstream hydrolysis protocol.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine-acetaldehyde condensation to threonine-family products\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported conditions: pH 3.3-3.7, 60-80 degrees C, homogeneous solution without inorganic solids. Proposed integration test: 343 K, initially 10-50 mM measured free glycine and comparable acetaldehyde, sampled over 1-14 days; concentrations and duration are not reported values. Use an HCN-poor aqueous fraction from the upstream branch. For crude ensemble feeds, quantify pre-existing threonine and use labeled acetaldehyde in diagnostic experiments to distinguish new production from carryover. No natural selective glycine purification is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde/amino-acid condensation chemistry generates the required C-C connectivity within a broader product network. Imine-mediated chemistry is a plausible explanation; an efficient free-glycine enolate at acidic pH is not assumed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) detected threonine in reactions of glycine or oligoglycine with aldehydes at these pH and temperature ranges, without inorganic solids. The experiment was poorly selective, and its supplied summary gives neither an isolated threonine yield nor a threonine/allothreonine ratio. Allothreonine is retained as a chemically expected competing product, not assigned a measured abundance. Application to crude glycine-bearing hydrolysates is an untested integration. Koga and Naraoka (2017) further show why generic aldehyde/ammonia chemistry must not be substituted for this amino-acid-dependent branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-ammonia oligomerization and hydrolytic amino-acid release ensemble\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ammonia/cyanide basic medium during oligomerization\",\n        \"Hydronium during the subsequent hydrolysis stage\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K during oligomerization; 350-370 K for proposed environmental release\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"stage-dependent general-base promotion, then acid-catalyzed hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed first-stage screen: 0.1-1 M total cyanide, pH 9-10, 7-30 days. Retain both suspended residue and dissolved material, then transfer measured fractions to a separate acidic reservoir. Proposed environmental release: pH 1.5, 363 K, sampled over 1-30 days. Historical strong-HCl hydrolysis around 373 K for many hours is a laboratory recovery benchmark, not evidence that the proposed milder stage completes. Measure free amino acids before and after each stage and after a separate analytical hydrolysis. Subsequent use in rxn_013 requires pH adjustment, direct glycine measurement and explicit dilution accounting.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A heterogeneous HCN oligomer network generates hydrolyzable amino-acid-bearing material. Its molecular threonine precursor is unresolved. NH3 and CO2 are formal net hydrolysis/redox coproducts; their inferred coefficients are not measured ensemble stoichiometry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lowe et al. (1963) and Matthews and Moser (1967), together with the related Matthews and Moser studies summarized in the research, support amino acids including threonine in hydrolysates of HCN-derived material. The node deliberately does not identify that material with the defined aminomalononitrile adduct. High cyanide concentration, recovery from heterogeneous phases and effective environmental hydrolysis remain unresolved. The proposed handling sequence makes those dependencies explicit but does not establish sustained free-threonine or free-glycine availability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Anoxic HCN radiolysis and hydrolytic amino-acid release ensemble\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium during post-irradiation hydrolysis only\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K during irradiation; 350-370 K for proposed environmental release\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"none during irradiation; hydronium during hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported activation: oxygen-free 0.004-0.1 M aqueous HCN and a laboratory cobalt-60 dose rate near 2 kGy per hour. A proposed 20 kGy comparison exposure would take 10 h at that rate; 20 kGy is a screening choice, not a published minimum productive dose. A natural site delivering D Gy per year would require 20000/D years for that exposure. For illustration, assumed doses of 0.001-1 Gy per year imply 20 million to 20000 years; no such site dose or precursor lifetime is established here. After irradiation, exclude further exposure and test pH 1.5 at 363 K for 1-30 days, separately from stronger analytical hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water-derived and cyanide-derived radicals generate an unresolved oligomeric product ensemble. Subsequent hydrolysis releases amino acids. The listed NH3 and CO2 provide possible formal net conservation closure, not an identified radical sequence or measured gas distribution.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Sweeney et al. (1976) and Dragani\u0107 et al. (1977) independently support threonine and glycine recovery after irradiation and hydrolysis; Dragani\u0107 et al. explicitly identify oligomeric precursors and approximately racemic chiral products. A cobalt-60 source is not assigned to the early Earth. The dose-time calculation exposes rather than resolves the natural dosimetry problem. This branch is a laboratory-supported accessibility route with unestablished environmental completion; continued irradiation could also destroy its products.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"H2S-rich wet electrical-discharge amino-acid ensemble\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Tungsten electrode surfaces in the laboratory benchmark; catalytic involvement is unresolved\",\n        \"Hydronium only if a separate hydrolytic-release stage is applied\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K in aqueous reservoirs; nonthermal discharge is a separate energy source\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar total; benchmark gas partial pressures are listed below\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"no intentionally added mineral; possible electrode/sulfur effects unresolved\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Benchmark apparatus: approximately 5 L, with CH4/H2S/NH3/CO2 partial pressures of 258/100/250/87 mmHg plus water vapor. Proposed implementation uses a warm water source, a cooled 300-310 K condensate collector and a 7-day collection test; the duration is a design assumption. Discharge power and integrated energy are not provided in the supplied summary and must be metered rather than invented. Analyze untreated condensate for free amino acids, then separately assay hydrolyzable material. A candidate release test is pH 1.5 at 363 K for 1-30 days, with no presumed equivalence to archival workup. Natural deployment requires a local reducing plume and measured gas-water capture, not this composition globally.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electrical discharge and gas-water cycling generate a complex organic ensemble. The threonine-forming molecular sequence is unresolved; no specific Strecker or aminomalononitrile mechanism is assigned. H2 and elemental sulfur are formal net redox closure products, not quantified yields.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Parker et al. (2011) identified threonine and a broad amino-acid inventory in the archived H2S-rich Miller experiment. This establishes product accessibility but not the time-resolved concentration of free threonine in a natural collection pool. Sulfur deposition near tungsten electrodes limits direct translation to lightning. Cleaves et al. (2008) do not establish an equivalent threonine endpoint in a neutral atmosphere, so their work is not used to remove the reducing-gas requirement. Using the glycine fraction in rxn_013 is a separate, untested coupling.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry methane-ammonia discharge followed by aqueous residue hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium during the subsequent aqueous hydrolysis stage\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K at residue collection surfaces; 350-370 K for proposed aqueous release\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar as a proposed collection setting\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"none assigned during discharge; hydronium during hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"The activation stage is dry CH4/NH3 discharge; water in the input list is introduced only afterward. Collect deposited organic residue separately before wetting. A 7-day collection period is proposed for a diagnostic experiment, but the supplied summary does not specify the original gas ratio, pressure, electrode material, deposited energy or residue collection efficiency. Those omissions prevent a claim of reproduction. Proposed environmental release is pH 1.5 at 363 K for 1-30 days, compared with separate strong-acid analytical recovery. A naturally dry reducing discharge pocket followed by transport to acidic water is a hypothesis, not a documented site.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dry energetic processing produces unresolved nitrogen-rich organic residues; subsequent hydration and hydrolysis can release threonine. The combined node is a two-stage ensemble, not an elementary reaction. H2 is a formal reducing-equivalent coproduct for the projected net equation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Matthews and Moser (1966), as summarized in the supplied research, support threonine release after hydrolysis of dry methane/ammonia discharge residues. They do not establish the natural residue production, collection or hydrolysis sequence proposed here. This is therefore a lower-confidence implementation with product-level laboratory support and unresolved environmental operations, not a demonstrated free-threonine source.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde-to-lactaldehyde photoredox/Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde and HCN form lactonitrile; Cu(I)/sulfide photochemistry supplies lactaldehyde, which passes through its cyanohydrin and aminonitrile to threonine-family amino acids. Precursor formation is experimentally supported, including the reported 19% lactaldehyde result. Environmental nitrile and amide hydrolysis remain unvalidated completion steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-trimer acetaldehyde-addition route\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN supplies aminomalononitrile, which adds acetaldehyde before hydrolysis and decarboxylation. This is a chemically distinct route with direct laboratory threonine/allothreonine evidence. Selective trimer availability and product release under the proposed milder acidic conditions are unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde, ammonia and HCN generate the glycine skeleton through aminoacetonitrile; hydrolysis supplies free glycine for the warm, weakly acidic acetaldehyde reaction. The upstream chemistry is chemically standard but its specified integration and environmental hydrolysis have not been demonstrated by the supplied threonine studies.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_018\",\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-oligomer-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation\",\n      \"description\": \"Track the glycine output of the HCN oligomer/hydrolysis ensemble into rxn_013, rather than its directly recovered threonine output. Newly formed threonine must be distinguished from pre-existing threonine, for example by acetaldehyde-label incorporation in diagnostic experiments. Free-glycine availability, crude-mixture compatibility and environmental hydrolysis are unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Radiolytically derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation\",\n      \"description\": \"Track glycine released from irradiated HCN-derived material into the separately supported glycine/acetaldehyde reaction. This is a graph-distinct coupling, not an independently demonstrated radiation-to-threonine mechanism. Natural absorbed dose, precursor survival, hydrolysis and new-product attribution must all be established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Wet-discharge-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the measured free-glycine fraction of collected discharge products as the substrate for rxn_013 after pH and concentration adjustment. Threonine already present in the discharge inventory is not credited to this downstream reaction. Gas-water capture, energy exposure and compatibility of the crude glycine stream remain untested.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct threonine recovery from an HCN-ammonia oligomer ensemble\",\n      \"description\": \"Follow threonine-bearing material formed within bulk HCN/ammonia chemistry to hydrolytic release, without assigning aminomalononitrile as its unique precursor. Historical hydrolysate detection supports accessibility. Completion in the proposed acidic surface reservoir, rather than in strong laboratory hydrolysis, is not established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct threonine recovery from an irradiated HCN ensemble\",\n      \"description\": \"Follow the threonine-bearing fraction generated by anoxic cyanide radiolysis through post-irradiation hydrolysis. Laboratory product detection is supported independently, but no natural dose source or environmental release rate sufficient to operate this pathway has been demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Threonine-bearing products from an H2S-rich wet discharge\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the experimentally detected threonine output of the wet reducing-gas discharge ensemble without assigning a resolved molecular mechanism. A local reducing gas source, condensate capture, measured energy exposure and free-versus-hydrolyzable product analysis are required before interpreting this as an operating environmental source.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry reducing-gas discharge residue followed by threonine release\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate organic residue by dry methane/ammonia discharge, collect it, and introduce water only in the subsequent release stage. Laboratory hydrolysate evidence supports the endpoint, whereas the natural dry-gas setting, collection efficiency, energy budget and sufficiently effective environmental hydrolysis remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_019\",\n    \"mol_020\",\n    \"mol_021\",\n    \"mol_022\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "Construct a connected, surface-based network with 22 molecule nodes, 17 reaction nodes and 10 graph-distinct pathways. The target is racemic threonine, the (2R,3S)/(2S,3R) pair, not enantiopure L-threonine; allothreonine is represented separately. The best-resolved branches are lactaldehyde/Strecker chemistry, aminomalononitrile/acetaldehyde chemistry and glycine/acetaldehyde chemistry. Four additional reaction nodes represent experimentally supported but molecularly unresolved reaction ensembles. Three pathways connect glycine recovered from those ensembles to the separately demonstrated glycine/acetaldehyde reaction. These are ten distinct network routes, not ten independently demonstrated threonine mechanisms. Cyanide-rich preparation, near-neutral photochemistry and acidic hydrolysis occur in separate staged reservoirs; no single pond is assumed to support incompatible conditions simultaneously. Numerical conditions explicitly identified as proposed are test conditions, not reported experimental results or established natural operating conditions. Strong-acid analytical recovery is distinguished from proposed environmental release throughout. Consequently, this is a complete candidate reaction graph, not a demonstration that ten environmental processes sustain free-threonine production. Natural radiation dose, discharge energy, intermediate transfer, hydrolysis rates and formation-versus-destruction kinetics remain unresolved where stated. Unresolved ensembles are represented by staged net reaction nodes rather than fictitious polymer molecules. Their CO2, H2, NH3 and sulfur outputs provide formal conservation closure, not measured product distributions. Inferred coefficients do not establish yields or stereoisomer ratios. Neutral parent formulas are used for pH-dependent species; protonation and spectator counterions are handled in conditions. Null environment_formed identifies externally supplied feedstocks, including species also regenerated internally. Coproducts use the permitted intermediate role. No molecular oxygen, unjustified mineral catalyst, cryogenic extraterrestrial step or hot-vent threonine-production step is introduced.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "lactonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H5NO/c1-3(5)2-4/h3,5H,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "lactaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "lactaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "threonine-family aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide", "common_name": "threonine-family aminoamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "rac-(2R,3S)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-threonine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "rac-(2R,3R)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-allothreonine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H3N3", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanedinitrile", "common_name": "aminomalononitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H7N3O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedinitrile", "common_name": "aminomalononitrile-acetaldehyde addition product", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H9NO5", "iupac_name": "2-amino-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedioic acid", "common_name": "hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed operating window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, near-neutral receiving pool; proposed pH 7-8 and 10-30 mM total concentrations of each substrate. Sample over 0.5-24 h rather than assuming quantitative conversion. These concentration and residence-time ranges are design assumptions, not a quoted lactonitrile preparation. Volatile feedstocks require continuing supply or demonstrable retention; equilibrium includes free acetaldehyde, HCN and their hydrated or ionized forms."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to the acetaldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer forms racemic lactonitrile reversibly.", "reasoning": "This is the chemically identified entry to the threonine-specific reductive homologation branch in Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). No mineral is required for cyanohydrin formation. Simultaneous aldehyde and cyanide availability, rather than carbon counting alone, is necessary. The pool formulation above remains an operating proposal."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Copper-assisted sulfide photoreduction of lactonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN-derived Cu(I)-cyanide complexes, photoredox catalyst", "Phosphate buffer, pH-control agent rather than catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed temperature-controlled photochemical stage", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "Benchmark activation: 254 nm irradiation for 2 h. Proposed starting formulation: 10 mM lactonitrile, 30 mM H2S, 33 mM phosphate, nominal 1 mM CuCN and pH 7. These concentrations are transferred from the representative glycolonitrile experiment, not claimed as the exact reported lactonitrile recipe. Use a shallow optical path and measure transmitted UV and absorbed fluence; two laboratory hours cannot be equated to two hours of sunlight. Allow sulfur-rich solids to settle before downstream processing, while measuring dissolved copper, sulfide and organic recovery."}, "mechanism": "Photoredox reduction of the nitrile through an imine-level intermediate, followed by hydrolysis, yields lactaldehyde and ammonia. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. Elemental sulfur represents a possible terminal oxidized-sulfur product for net electron balance; actual sulfur and polysulfide speciation is unresolved here.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland (2013) reported 19% lactaldehyde after 2 h and subsequent access to the threonine-family aminonitrile. The 42% glycolaldehyde result from their representative formulation is not assigned to lactaldehyde. Copper is included because it is supported in this implementation, not because every sulfide photoreduction requires it. Ranjan et al. (2018) identify sulfide precipitation and sulfur-related UV attenuation as major deployment constraints. Fe2+-rich water therefore cannot be assumed to preserve the nominal dissolved sulfide and copper concentrations."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7-8.5 with additional cyanide supplied to the cooled lactaldehyde-containing photolysate. A 0.5-2 day sampling window is proposed, not reported as a completion time. Transfer promptly after rxn_002; measure aldehyde loss during settling, transport and cyanide addition. No purification or quantitative recovery of lactaldehyde is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde gives 2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile, with multiple stereoisomers possible.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland (2013) describe lactaldehyde conversion through its cyanohydrin before aminonitrile formation. Explicitly retaining this species avoids treating the entire Strecker sequence as instantaneous. Lactaldehyde hydration, competing condensation and persistence in crude photolysate remain unquantified."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Excess ammonia in a separate, UV-shielded aqueous stage. Proposed pH 8-9 and 50-200 mM total ammonia; monitor over 7-28 days. The ammonia concentration is a design range, whereas days-to-weeks aminonitrile formation is supported at the reaction-class level. Account for ammonia and HCN volatilization and competing cyanide chemistry. Do not begin acidic hydrolysis until the aminonitrile inventory is measured."}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin re-equilibration exposes lactaldehyde; ammonia forms an imine or iminium species, which cyanide captures. The net equation is not direct nucleophilic displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland (2013) demonstrated conversion to the threonine/allothreonine Strecker aminonitrile using HCN and excess ammonia. Their work supports the precursor, not an isolated free-threonine yield. This slow stage is a kinetic bottleneck, and neither threo selectivity nor L enrichment is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydration of the threonine-family aminonitrile to aminoamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by an HCl-bearing acidic surface reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed environmental hydrolysis window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed test point: pH 1.5 and 363 K, sampled over 1-30 days. These conditions are not demonstrated completion conditions for this substrate. Transfer from rxn_004 requires a measured acid-equivalent budget to overcome ammonia and mineral buffering. Use a separate acidic reservoir; no instantaneous pH switch in one pond is assumed. Strong-HCl hydrolysis near 373 K is a laboratory comparator, not an environmental step silently substituted here."}, "mechanism": "Acid-promoted nitrile hydration produces the corresponding aminoamide; protonated species dominate under these conditions but are represented by neutral parent formulas.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically justified but environmentally unvalidated extension beyond the precursor endpoint of Ritson and Sutherland (2013). The aminoamide is a mechanistically expected intermediate, not a separately isolated product claimed from that study. Substrate-specific hydration rates under the proposed conditions are missing, so this edge carries unknown environmental flux."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Aminoamide hydrolysis and release of threonine-family amino acids", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium in the acidic hydrolysis reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K during proposed hydrolysis; 300-310 K after collection", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue the pH 1.5, 363 K candidate time course from rxn_005, with separate quantification of nitrile, amide, threonine and allothreonine. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium. Cool collected aliquots promptly. A 30-day observation window is proposed, not a claim of conversion. Determine net accumulation from formation and loss measured in the same fluid; do not assume temperatures below 394 K eliminate degradation."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the carboxamide releases ammonia and the corresponding amino acid. The stereoisomeric precursor pool can yield both racemic threonine and racemic allothreonine; no fixed ratio is imposed.", "reasoning": "General amide hydrolysis supports the transformation, but the supplied threonine-specific photoredox studies do not establish its environmental rate or free-product yield. This is therefore an explicitly unvalidated completion step relative to Ritson and Sutherland (2013). The reported hydrothermal transformation of threonine at 394-433 K, in 'Threonine Transformation under Hydrothermal Conditions' (2005), motivates avoiding hot vents but does not supply a safe-temperature threshold or a low-temperature destruction rate."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "HCN oligomerization to aminomalononitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ammonia/cyanide basic medium, promoting HCN oligomerization"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "general-base promotion by the ammonia/cyanide medium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed concentrated alkaline preparation: 0.1-1 M total cyanide, pH 8-9, sampled from 6 h to 7 days. These are screening conditions, not a reported selective aminomalononitrile yield. Capture or transfer an aminomalononitrile-containing fraction before extensive higher oligomerization. A selective natural separation is not assumed. Cyanide concentration and retention must be established independently."}, "mechanism": "Stepwise HCN oligomerization accesses its trimer, aminomalononitrile, within a competing oligomer network.", "reasoning": "Aminomalononitrile is the HCN trimer used by Thanassi (1975); the broader competing HCN oligomer chemistry is supported by Lowe et al. (1963) and Matthews and Moser (1967). These sources do not establish a selective environmental HCN-to-trimer process at the proposed conditions. Toner and Catling (2019) provide a cyanide concentration framework, not a guarantee of simultaneous trimer accumulation and acetaldehyde supply."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Carbon-carbon addition of aminomalononitrile to acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed ambient-temperature implementation", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately neutral water, proposed pH 6.5-7.5. Test 5-20 mM aminomalononitrile and comparable acetaldehyde over 0.5-7 days. Concentrations and residence times are proposed because the supplied summary does not provide the original values. Adjust the alkaline feed from rxn_007 before aldehyde addition and quantify trimer loss and competing oligomers during that adjustment."}, "mechanism": "Reaction at the activated central carbon of aminomalononitrile forms a C-C bond to acetaldehyde, generating the hydroxyethyl-substituted dinitrile.", "reasoning": "Thanassi (1975) directly supports acetaldehyde/aminomalononitrile chemistry yielding DL-threonine and allothreonine after hydrolysis. Kuroda et al. (2020) describe the C-C-forming stage as nearly neutral aqueous chemistry. No mineral catalyst is justified. The defined adduct is a mechanistic representation of the productive branch, not an assertion that the crude mixture contains only this species."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrolysis of the substituted aminomalononitrile", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium in an HCl-bearing acidic reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed environmental test window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2, with a test point of pH 1.5 and 363 K over 1-30 days. Quantify both nitrile groups, intermediate amides and released ammonia. The supplied literature summary establishes laboratory acid hydrolysis but does not give the exact original acid concentration and residence time. Those missing values are not invented or equated with this candidate environmental stage."}, "mechanism": "Sequential hydration and hydrolysis of both nitrile groups gives a substituted aminomalonic acid and ammonia. The dicarboxylic acid may be transient because decarboxylation can accompany hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Acid hydrolysis followed by decarboxylation is supported as the product-releasing sequence by Thanassi (1975) and its discussion in Kuroda et al. (2020). The separate dicarboxylic-acid node resolves the mechanism but is not claimed as an isolated intermediate in those experiments. Completion at pH 1-2 and 350-370 K remains unvalidated; strong laboratory hydrolysis cannot establish its environmental rate."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Decarboxylation of hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid", "inputs": ["mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K during release; cool promptly afterward", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic aqueous conditions inherited from rxn_009, proposed pH 1-3. Monitor decarboxylation during the same 1-30 day test rather than assigning instantaneous conversion. Measure threonine/allothreonine formation and loss independently. CO2 removal may favor net decarboxylation, but neither a removal rate nor a product yield is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Loss of one carboxyl group from the substituted aminomalonic acid produces the alpha-amino-acid skeleton. Protonation generates stereoisomeric threonine-family products.", "reasoning": "Thanassi (1975) demonstrated DL-threonine and allothreonine after the overall hydrolysis/decarboxylation sequence. This supplies stronger product-level evidence than the photoredox precursor endpoint, but the supplied research does not resolve the decarboxylation rate under the proposed environmental conditions. No numerical yield or stereoisomer ratio is inferred."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed upstream glycine preparation", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9, 10-30 mM formaldehyde and total cyanide, and 50-200 mM total ammonia. Sample over 1-14 days. These values define a testable module, not a protocol supplied by the threonine papers. Monitor formaldehyde sequestration into competing ammonia adducts and cyanohydrins. Use a separate cyanide-rich stage before acidic hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde and ammonia form imine-level species that undergo cyanide addition to aminoacetonitrile; cyanohydrin equilibria can participate.", "reasoning": "Formaldehyde Strecker chemistry is a standard chemically justified route to the glycine skeleton. Nevertheless, this particular operating module and its integration with threonine production are speculative relative to the supplied evidence: Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015) support related aminonitrile chemistry, not the exact proposed glycine preparation. The edge is included to make the glycine-dependent branch explicit rather than importing glycine as an unexplained complex feedstock."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium in an acidic hydrolysis reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed environmental release window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2 and 1-30 day observation window, with pH 1.5 and 363 K as a test point. Measure glycine directly before any stronger analytical hydrolysis. For transfer to rxn_013, cool, determine remaining cyanide and ammonia, and adjust the measured solution to pH 3.3-3.7. Required dilution and resulting glycine concentration must be recorded."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile hydration followed by amide hydrolysis releases glycine and ammonia; the transient glycinamide is not separately inventoried.", "reasoning": "This is standard nitrile-to-amino-acid chemistry, but the supplied literature does not validate the proposed environmental time course. It is an explicitly unvalidated integration step, not evidence that glycine recovered by strong analytical hydrolysis was already freely available. Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) support the subsequent glycine/aldehyde chemistry, not this upstream hydrolysis protocol."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Glycine-acetaldehyde condensation to threonine-family products", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "333-353 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported conditions: pH 3.3-3.7, 60-80 degrees C, homogeneous solution without inorganic solids. Proposed integration test: 343 K, initially 10-50 mM measured free glycine and comparable acetaldehyde, sampled over 1-14 days; concentrations and duration are not reported values. Use an HCN-poor aqueous fraction from the upstream branch. For crude ensemble feeds, quantify pre-existing threonine and use labeled acetaldehyde in diagnostic experiments to distinguish new production from carryover. No natural selective glycine purification is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Aldehyde/amino-acid condensation chemistry generates the required C-C connectivity within a broader product network. Imine-mediated chemistry is a plausible explanation; an efficient free-glycine enolate at acidic pH is not assumed.", "reasoning": "Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) detected threonine in reactions of glycine or oligoglycine with aldehydes at these pH and temperature ranges, without inorganic solids. The experiment was poorly selective, and its supplied summary gives neither an isolated threonine yield nor a threonine/allothreonine ratio. Allothreonine is retained as a chemically expected competing product, not assigned a measured abundance. Application to crude glycine-bearing hydrolysates is an untested integration. Koga and Naraoka (2017) further show why generic aldehyde/ammonia chemistry must not be substituted for this amino-acid-dependent branch."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "HCN-ammonia oligomerization and hydrolytic amino-acid release ensemble", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ammonia/cyanide basic medium during oligomerization", "Hydronium during the subsequent hydrolysis stage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K during oligomerization; 350-370 K for proposed environmental release", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "stage-dependent general-base promotion, then acid-catalyzed hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed first-stage screen: 0.1-1 M total cyanide, pH 9-10, 7-30 days. Retain both suspended residue and dissolved material, then transfer measured fractions to a separate acidic reservoir. Proposed environmental release: pH 1.5, 363 K, sampled over 1-30 days. Historical strong-HCl hydrolysis around 373 K for many hours is a laboratory recovery benchmark, not evidence that the proposed milder stage completes. Measure free amino acids before and after each stage and after a separate analytical hydrolysis. Subsequent use in rxn_013 requires pH adjustment, direct glycine measurement and explicit dilution accounting."}, "mechanism": "A heterogeneous HCN oligomer network generates hydrolyzable amino-acid-bearing material. Its molecular threonine precursor is unresolved. NH3 and CO2 are formal net hydrolysis/redox coproducts; their inferred coefficients are not measured ensemble stoichiometry.", "reasoning": "Lowe et al. (1963) and Matthews and Moser (1967), together with the related Matthews and Moser studies summarized in the research, support amino acids including threonine in hydrolysates of HCN-derived material. The node deliberately does not identify that material with the defined aminomalononitrile adduct. High cyanide concentration, recovery from heterogeneous phases and effective environmental hydrolysis remain unresolved. The proposed handling sequence makes those dependencies explicit but does not establish sustained free-threonine or free-glycine availability."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Anoxic HCN radiolysis and hydrolytic amino-acid release ensemble", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium during post-irradiation hydrolysis only"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K during irradiation; 350-370 K for proposed environmental release", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "none during irradiation; hydronium during hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported activation: oxygen-free 0.004-0.1 M aqueous HCN and a laboratory cobalt-60 dose rate near 2 kGy per hour. A proposed 20 kGy comparison exposure would take 10 h at that rate; 20 kGy is a screening choice, not a published minimum productive dose. A natural site delivering D Gy per year would require 20000/D years for that exposure. For illustration, assumed doses of 0.001-1 Gy per year imply 20 million to 20000 years; no such site dose or precursor lifetime is established here. After irradiation, exclude further exposure and test pH 1.5 at 363 K for 1-30 days, separately from stronger analytical hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Water-derived and cyanide-derived radicals generate an unresolved oligomeric product ensemble. Subsequent hydrolysis releases amino acids. The listed NH3 and CO2 provide possible formal net conservation closure, not an identified radical sequence or measured gas distribution.", "reasoning": "Sweeney et al. (1976) and Dragani\u0107 et al. (1977) independently support threonine and glycine recovery after irradiation and hydrolysis; Dragani\u0107 et al. explicitly identify oligomeric precursors and approximately racemic chiral products. A cobalt-60 source is not assigned to the early Earth. The dose-time calculation exposes rather than resolves the natural dosimetry problem. This branch is a laboratory-supported accessibility route with unestablished environmental completion; continued irradiation could also destroy its products."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "H2S-rich wet electrical-discharge amino-acid ensemble", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_002", "mol_017", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_012", "mol_021", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Tungsten electrode surfaces in the laboratory benchmark; catalytic involvement is unresolved", "Hydronium only if a separate hydrolytic-release stage is applied"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K in aqueous reservoirs; nonthermal discharge is a separate energy source", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar total; benchmark gas partial pressures are listed below", "catalyst": "no intentionally added mineral; possible electrode/sulfur effects unresolved", "aqueous": true, "other": "Benchmark apparatus: approximately 5 L, with CH4/H2S/NH3/CO2 partial pressures of 258/100/250/87 mmHg plus water vapor. Proposed implementation uses a warm water source, a cooled 300-310 K condensate collector and a 7-day collection test; the duration is a design assumption. Discharge power and integrated energy are not provided in the supplied summary and must be metered rather than invented. Analyze untreated condensate for free amino acids, then separately assay hydrolyzable material. A candidate release test is pH 1.5 at 363 K for 1-30 days, with no presumed equivalence to archival workup. Natural deployment requires a local reducing plume and measured gas-water capture, not this composition globally."}, "mechanism": "Electrical discharge and gas-water cycling generate a complex organic ensemble. The threonine-forming molecular sequence is unresolved; no specific Strecker or aminomalononitrile mechanism is assigned. H2 and elemental sulfur are formal net redox closure products, not quantified yields.", "reasoning": "Parker et al. (2011) identified threonine and a broad amino-acid inventory in the archived H2S-rich Miller experiment. This establishes product accessibility but not the time-resolved concentration of free threonine in a natural collection pool. Sulfur deposition near tungsten electrodes limits direct translation to lightning. Cleaves et al. (2008) do not establish an equivalent threonine endpoint in a neutral atmosphere, so their work is not used to remove the reducing-gas requirement. Using the glycine fraction in rxn_013 is a separate, untested coupling."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Dry methane-ammonia discharge followed by aqueous residue hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium during the subsequent aqueous hydrolysis stage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K at residue collection surfaces; 350-370 K for proposed aqueous release", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar as a proposed collection setting", "catalyst": "none assigned during discharge; hydronium during hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "The activation stage is dry CH4/NH3 discharge; water in the input list is introduced only afterward. Collect deposited organic residue separately before wetting. A 7-day collection period is proposed for a diagnostic experiment, but the supplied summary does not specify the original gas ratio, pressure, electrode material, deposited energy or residue collection efficiency. Those omissions prevent a claim of reproduction. Proposed environmental release is pH 1.5 at 363 K for 1-30 days, compared with separate strong-acid analytical recovery. A naturally dry reducing discharge pocket followed by transport to acidic water is a hypothesis, not a documented site."}, "mechanism": "Dry energetic processing produces unresolved nitrogen-rich organic residues; subsequent hydration and hydrolysis can release threonine. The combined node is a two-stage ensemble, not an elementary reaction. H2 is a formal reducing-equivalent coproduct for the projected net equation.", "reasoning": "Matthews and Moser (1966), as summarized in the supplied research, support threonine release after hydrolysis of dry methane/ammonia discharge residues. They do not establish the natural residue production, collection or hydrolysis sequence proposed here. This is therefore a lower-confidence implementation with product-level laboratory support and unresolved environmental operations, not a demonstrated free-threonine source."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Acetaldehyde-to-lactaldehyde photoredox/Strecker route", "description": "Acetaldehyde and HCN form lactonitrile; Cu(I)/sulfide photochemistry supplies lactaldehyde, which passes through its cyanohydrin and aminonitrile to threonine-family amino acids. Precursor formation is experimentally supported, including the reported 19% lactaldehyde result. Environmental nitrile and amide hydrolysis remain unvalidated completion steps.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "HCN-trimer acetaldehyde-addition route", "description": "HCN supplies aminomalononitrile, which adds acetaldehyde before hydrolysis and decarboxylation. This is a chemically distinct route with direct laboratory threonine/allothreonine evidence. Selective trimer availability and product release under the proposed milder acidic conditions are unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation", "description": "Formaldehyde, ammonia and HCN generate the glycine skeleton through aminoacetonitrile; hydrolysis supplies free glycine for the warm, weakly acidic acetaldehyde reaction. The upstream chemistry is chemically standard but its specified integration and environmental hydrolysis have not been demonstrated by the supplied threonine studies.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_018", "mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "HCN-oligomer-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation", "description": "Track the glycine output of the HCN oligomer/hydrolysis ensemble into rxn_013, rather than its directly recovered threonine output. Newly formed threonine must be distinguished from pre-existing threonine, for example by acetaldehyde-label incorporation in diagnostic experiments. Free-glycine availability, crude-mixture compatibility and environmental hydrolysis are unvalidated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_014", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Radiolytically derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation", "description": "Track glycine released from irradiated HCN-derived material into the separately supported glycine/acetaldehyde reaction. This is a graph-distinct coupling, not an independently demonstrated radiation-to-threonine mechanism. Natural absorbed dose, precursor survival, hydrolysis and new-product attribution must all be established.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_015", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Wet-discharge-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde condensation", "description": "Use the measured free-glycine fraction of collected discharge products as the substrate for rxn_013 after pH and concentration adjustment. Threonine already present in the discharge inventory is not credited to this downstream reaction. Gas-water capture, energy exposure and compatibility of the crude glycine stream remain untested.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_016", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Direct threonine recovery from an HCN-ammonia oligomer ensemble", "description": "Follow threonine-bearing material formed within bulk HCN/ammonia chemistry to hydrolytic release, without assigning aminomalononitrile as its unique precursor. Historical hydrolysate detection supports accessibility. Completion in the proposed acidic surface reservoir, rather than in strong laboratory hydrolysis, is not established.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Direct threonine recovery from an irradiated HCN ensemble", "description": "Follow the threonine-bearing fraction generated by anoxic cyanide radiolysis through post-irradiation hydrolysis. Laboratory product detection is supported independently, but no natural dose source or environmental release rate sufficient to operate this pathway has been demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Threonine-bearing products from an H2S-rich wet discharge", "description": "Use the experimentally detected threonine output of the wet reducing-gas discharge ensemble without assigning a resolved molecular mechanism. A local reducing gas source, condensate capture, measured energy exposure and free-versus-hydrolyzable product analysis are required before interpreting this as an operating environmental source.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Dry reducing-gas discharge residue followed by threonine release", "description": "Generate organic residue by dry methane/ammonia discharge, collect it, and introduce water only in the subsequent release stage. Laboratory hydrolysate evidence supports the endpoint, whereas the natural dry-gas setting, collection efficiency, energy budget and sufficiently effective environmental hydrolysis remain unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": []}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_017", "mol_019", "mol_020", "mol_021", "mol_022"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_019", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_012", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_013"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_012", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_013", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_013", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_013"], "outgoing_reactions": []